# minimum required Qore version
%requires qore >= 1.14

# requires the SqlUtil module
%requires SqlUtil >= 3.10
%requires json

# don't use "$" signs for variables and class members, assume local variable scope
%modern

# require type definitions everywhere

# enable all warnings

module PgsqlSqlUtilBase {
    version = "1.19";
    desc = "base user module for working with PostgreSQL SQL data";
    author = "David Nichols <david@qore.org>";
    url = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
}

# version history is included below in the docs

/** @mainpage PgsqlSqlUtilBase Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section pgsqlsqlutilbaseintro PgsqlSqlUtilBase Module Introduction

    The %PgsqlSqlUtilBase module provides a high level API for working with PostgreSQL database objects and provides
    the implementation for the \c jdbc and \c odbc SqlUtil modules supporting PostgreSQL.

    Usually this module is loaded on demand from the SqlUtil module, however to use this module
    directly, use \c "%requires PgsqlSqlUtilBase" in your code.

    All the public symbols in the module are defined in the PgsqlSqlUtilBase namespace

    @section pgsql_schema_scope Schema Scope of Object Names

    PostgreSQL resolves an object name without a schema against the connection's \c search_path.  This module
    resolves such a name for tables, views, sequences, functions, and types in <tt>current_schema()</tt>, the first
    existing schema of the \c search_path (or \c "public" if the \c search_path names no existing schema); a name
    of the form \c "schema.name" is resolved in the schema given.  The schema and the name can be double-quoted
    identifiers, as in SQL, which can have any character, \c "." too, with a quote doubled
    (ex: <tt>"my schema"."My Table"</tt>, <tt>"a.b"."c.d"</tt>, <tt>s."F"</tt>); a quoted identifier is looked up
    without its quotes and with its case, an unquoted identifier is looked up as given.  A table is named in SQL as
    its name gives it, and the schema that the \c search_path resolves a name without a schema to is quoted in SQL
    if it needs quotes; a part of a name that is not a double-quoted identifier and not an ordinary identifier
    (ex: <tt>my table</tt>, or a name with a quote or a semicolon) is given in double quotes in SQL, each double quote
    doubled, so that it names exactly that object and can never change the SQL (see @ref sql_identifiers).  The trigger functions of a table are the functions that its triggers execute, also with
    trigger arguments, named as the triggers name them: by the function name alone when the \c search_path resolves
    it to the function, otherwise with its schema, with each identifier that needs it quoted as PostgreSQL quotes it
    (ex: <tt>"my schema"."F"()</tt>).  The partitions of a table in <tt>current_schema()</tt> are named by their names
    alone, as other objects there are.

    Like Oracle and MySQL, which list only the objects of the connection's own schema, the following methods
    list only the objects in the schemas of the connection's \c search_path (<tt>current_schemas(false)</tt>),
    never every schema in the database:
    - @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::listTables() "AbstractDatabase::listTables()" and
      @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::tableIterator() "AbstractDatabase::tableIterator()"
    - @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::listViews() "AbstractDatabase::listViews()" and
      @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::viewIterator() "AbstractDatabase::viewIterator()"
    - @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::listSequences() "AbstractDatabase::listSequences()" and
      @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::sequenceIterator() "AbstractDatabase::sequenceIterator()"
    - @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::listFunctions() "AbstractDatabase::listFunctions()" and
      @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::functionIterator() "AbstractDatabase::functionIterator()"
    - @ref PgsqlSqlUtilBase::PgsqlDatabase::listMaterializedViews() "PgsqlDatabase::listMaterializedViews()"
    - @ref PgsqlSqlUtilBase::PgsqlDatabase::listTypes() "PgsqlDatabase::listTypes()"

    An object in <tt>current_schema()</tt> is listed by its name alone, and an object in any other
    \c search_path schema as \c "schema.name", so that every name listed resolves to the object listed, and an
    object name present in more than one \c search_path schema is listed once per schema.  Partition child
    tables are not listed as tables, views are listed by
    @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::listTables() "listTables()" as well, as \c information_schema.tables
    reports them, and session temporary tables are listed only if \c pg_temp is named
    explicitly in the \c search_path.  If the \c search_path names no existing schema, these methods return an
    empty list.

    To list the objects of a schema outside the \c search_path, use a connection whose \c search_path names it.

    @section pgsql_schema_management Schema Management on PostgreSQL

    @subsection pgsql_type_mapping Type Mapping

    Column types are mapped from %Qore types as follows:

    <b>PostgreSQL Column Type Mappings</b>
    |!Generic Type Name|!PostgreSQL Type Used
    |\c float|\c double
    |\c integer|\c bigint
    |\c number|\c numeric
    |\c string|\c varchar
    |\c date|\c timestamp
    |\c binary|\c bytea
    |\c bool|\c boolean
    |@ref SqlUtil::BLOB|\c bytea
    |@ref SqlUtil::CHAR|\c char
    |@ref SqlUtil::CLOB|\c text
    |@ref SqlUtil::NUMERIC|\c numeric
    |@ref SqlUtil::VARCHAR|\c varchar

    To use other types, use the \c "native_type"
    @ref SqlUtil::AbstractTable::ColumnDescOptions "column description option" with the native PostgreSQL type name
    instead (under the \c "driver" and \c "pgsql" keys for schemas supporting multiple databases).

    @subsection pgsql_index_options PostgreSQL Index Options

    In addition to the common SqlUtil index options, PostgreSQL supports:
    - \c where: a nonempty SQL predicate defining a partial index
    - \c include: an ordered list of distinct non-key column names (PostgreSQL 11+)
    - \c nulls_not_distinct: treat NULL keys as equal; requires a unique index (PostgreSQL 15+)
    - \c order: per-key \c asc or \c desc values (btree only)
    - \c nulls: per-key \c first or \c last values (btree only)

    The default is ascending order with NULLs last; descending order defaults to NULLs first.
    Introspection, SQL generation, schema descriptions, and alignment preserve these options.
    Alignment uses PostgreSQL's read-only EXPLAIN output to compare stored and declared predicates,
    including server-inserted casts and NOT IN rewrites. It does not prove arbitrary logical equivalence.

    @par Example
    Enforce one active fulfillment per customer while covering the status lookup:
    @code{.py}
table.addIndex("uk_active_fulfillment", True, ("customer_id",), {
    "where": "status NOT IN ('completed', 'cancelled')",
    "include": ("status",),
    "nulls_not_distinct": True,
});
    @endcode

    @subsection pgsql_other_objects Additional Object Types Supported

    The following additional schema objects can be managed with %PgsqlSqlUtilBase:
    - @ref pgsql_types "types"

    @subsection pgsql_types Types

    The @ref schema_desc_hash takes an optional key, \c "types" that allows types in PostgreSQL schemas to be managed
    along with other objects.

    The \c "types" should be assigned to a hash, each key name is the name of the type, and the values are strings
    giving the type definition.

    The \c "types" key can go in the top level of the @ref schema_desc_hash for PostgreSQL-only schemas, or, for
    schemas targeting multiple database types, under the \c "driver" and \c "pgsql" keys as in the following example:

    @code{.py}
hash<auto> schema = {
    "driver": {
        "pgsql": {
            "types": {
                "info_record": "id numeric, ind numeric, status char",
            },
        },
    },
};
    @endcode

    @subsection pgsql_functions Function Handling

    @par Function Names and Matching
    PostgreSQL functions are identified by their name and arguments; the algorithm implemented in this
    module is fairly simple; the argument names and types are used when trying to find functions, so they
    must match almost exactly.\n\n
    The only exception to the exact matching algorithm is that some simple substitutions are done on
    user-supplied arguments to try and use the verbose type names as retrieved by
    <a href="http://www.postgresql.org/docs/9.2/static/functions-info.html">pg_catalog.pg_get_function_identity_arguments()</a>,
    which always returns verbose type names (ie \c "character varying" instead of \c "varchar").\n\n
    Substitutions are made according to the inverse value of @ref PgsqlSqlUtilBase::PgsqlTable::PgsqlNameMap "PgsqlNameMap".\n\n
    This is done so that a user-supplied function name like \c "update_queue(qkey varchar, qdata text)" will match the
    value returned by
    <a href="http://www.postgresql.org/docs/9.2/static/functions-info.html">pg_catalog.pg_get_function_identity_arguments()</a>,
    which would be \c "update_queue(character varying queue)"

    @par Schema Management with Functions
    There is some simple logic in the driver that attempts to reorder function definitions so that they match the
    format returned by
    <a href="http://www.postgresql.org/docs/9.2/static/functions-info.html">pg_catalog.pg_get_functiondef()</a>
    which requires the following string to follow the function name and arguments:
    - <tt><b>returns</b></tt> <i>type</i> <tt><b>language</b></tt> <i>lang</i> <tt><b>as $function$ </b></tt>
    .
    The logic in the module attempts to move any language and volatility declarations from the end of the declaration
    to the beginning.  Additionally, dollar-quoting is detected automatically and substituted with \c "$function$"
    automatically.\n\n
    If your function is reported as needing updating even though it's already in the database correctly, then the
    logic in this module needs to be update to provide more flexibility (please raise an issue here:
    https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issue).  Until the module can be enhanced, try the format as
    in the following example:
    @code{.py}
update_queue_data(qkey varchar, qdata text) returns integer language plpgsql as $function$
declare
  rowcount integer;
begin
  update queue_data set data = qdata, queue_data_status = 'R' where queuekey = qkey and queue_data_status = 'W';
  get diagnostics rowcount = row_count;
  return rowcount;
end;
$function$
    @endcode
    @note Future versions of the module may provide more intelligence to reformat user-supplied functions to match the
    format retrieved from the database, however currently functions should be provided in the format above in order
    to use functionality like
    @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::getAlignFunctionSql() "AbstractDatabase::getAlignFunctionSql()" for example.

    @section pgsql_relnotes Release Notes

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev119 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.19
    - Security: a name of a table, column, index, constraint, sequence, schema, tablespace, trigger, view or alias
      that is not an ordinary PostgreSQL identifier is given in double quotes, each double quote doubled, wherever
      SQL is generated, so that a name from untrusted data can never change the SQL; an ordinary name is given as
      before, so existing schemas and alignment do not change (see @ref sql_identifiers).  A sequence named in
      \c nextval() or \c currval() is given as a correctly escaped string literal, a string value with a backslash
      is given as an escape string, so that it is read as given whatever the value of
      \c standard_conforming_strings, and a quote in a date format is doubled.  An empty name, or one with a NUL
      character, is refused with \c IDENTIFIER-ERROR
    - fixed @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::reclaimSpace() "reclaimSpace()" with the \c tables option vacuuming every
      table of the database once for each table given, instead of the tables given
    - fixed aligning a table whose primary key or unique constraint has the template's columns under another name:
      the key was dropped, which cascaded to the foreign constraints that reference it, and added again (a unique
      constraint was added "using index" its dropped index, which failed); the key is now renamed in place, with
      <tt>ALTER INDEX ... RENAME</tt> for a primary key and <tt>ALTER TABLE ... RENAME CONSTRAINT</tt> for a unique
      constraint
    - fixed renaming a foreign or check constraint, which dropped it, for a foreign constraint with \c cascade, and
      added it under the new name: it is renamed in place with <tt>ALTER TABLE ... RENAME CONSTRAINT</tt>
    - foreign constraints have \c ON \c DELETE and \c ON \c UPDATE referential actions (see @ref fk_actions), which
      are created, read from \c pg_constraint and compared, so a table whose foreign constraints have the template's
      actions needs no SQL, and an action change drops and adds the constraint again
    - fixed functions and types being looked up in every schema of the database instead of the schema that the
      connection resolves unqualified names in (see @ref pgsql_schema_scope), as tables, views, and sequences are:
      @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::getFunction() "getFunction()" reported the same function in two schemas as
      two matches, @ref PgsqlSqlUtilBase::PgsqlDatabase::getType() "PgsqlDatabase::getType()" merged the attributes
      of the types of a name in every schema, and a table's trigger functions were read from the functions of their
      names in every schema, raising an error for a name with more than one function; a function or type name may
      now be given as \c "schema.name"
    - fixed @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::listFunctions() "listFunctions()" listing the functions of the
      \c public schema only, and @ref PgsqlSqlUtilBase::PgsqlDatabase::listTypes() "PgsqlDatabase::listTypes()"
      listing the types of every schema; both list the objects of the schemas of the connection's \c search_path,
      named as tables are
    - fixed trigger functions named with their schema or with a quoted identifier by the trigger that executes them
      (ex: <tt>execute function "my schema"."F"()</tt>, as PostgreSQL names a function that the \c search_path does
      not resolve) not being read, so that they were missing from the SQL that creates, aligns, and drops the table;
      such a trigger function is read from the schema the trigger names, and named as the trigger names it
    - fixed the case of quoted identifiers and string literals in the source of a trigger being lost when the trigger
      is described, and ignored when triggers are compared, so that a trigger was created again executing another
      function, and a trigger executing another function of a name differing only in case was not aligned
    - fixed a \c "(" in a quoted identifier of a function name (ex: <tt>"f(x"()</tt>) being read as the start of
      the arguments of the function
    - fixed names given with double-quoted identifiers (ex: <tt>"my schema"."My Table"</tt>, <tt>"a.b"."c.d"</tt>,
      <tt>s."F"</tt>) not being looked up in the schema they name, or not being found: tables, functions,
      sequences, views, types, and partitions given such names are looked up without the quotes (see
      @ref pgsql_schema_scope), and a table given such a name is named in SQL as given; the schema that the
      \c search_path resolves a table name without a schema to is quoted in SQL if it needs quotes
    - fixed the functions of triggers called with trigger arguments (ex: <tt>execute function f('x', 'a,b')</tt>)
      not being read, so that they were missing from the SQL that creates, aligns, and drops the table
    - fixed @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::getSequence() "getSequence()" describing every sequence with the default
      start, increment, and maximum, as it read them from columns that a sequence does not have; they are read from
      \c pg_sequence (PostgreSQL 10 and later) or from the sequence (earlier versions), with the minimum and the cycle
      option, which @ref PgsqlSqlUtilBase::PgsqlSequence "PgsqlSequence" has as the new members
      @ref PgsqlSqlUtilBase::PgsqlSequence::min "min" and @ref PgsqlSqlUtilBase::PgsqlSequence::cycle "cycle", and
      gives in the SQL that creates the sequence; schema alignment creates a sequence that does not exist, and never
      creates one again nor restarts it
    - schema alignment aligns the increment, minimum, maximum and cycle option of an existing sequence with
      <tt>ALTER SEQUENCE</tt>, which keeps its current value
      (@ref PgsqlSqlUtilBase::PgsqlSequence::getAlterSql() "PgsqlSequence::getAlterSql()"); the starting value is not
      changed, as that would reset the current value.  A new sequence is created with the minimum and cycle option
      of its description, and a maximum or minimum of 0 is a value, not the default
    - fixed a function source whose body is on the same line as its dollar quotes (ex:
      <tt>returns integer language sql as $$select 1$$</tt>) losing its body, so that the function was created
      without it and compared as different from the function in the database
    - fixed @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::getProcedure() "getProcedure()" raising \c RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR for a
      procedure that does not exist, so that a schema with a procedure that did not exist could not be aligned
    - Added the size of the current schema (\c current_schema(): the first existing schema of the connection's
      \c search_path; see @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::getCurrentSchemaPhysicalSize()
      "AbstractDatabase::getCurrentSchemaPhysicalSize()"), which measures only that schema's relations instead of
      the whole database with \c pg_database_size().
    - fixed a \c cast data provider expression with a size, or with a precision and a scale: the size was ignored
      and the precision was given as the scale, so that the cast failed or changed the value
    - Added offline table rewrite transactions that limit only lock waits on PostgreSQL 17 and later,
      preserving stricter session statement and transaction timeouts.
    - Added bounded direct-child partition catalog pages with parent-bound identity cursors,
      including nested and pending-detach identities without recursive inspection.
    - a negative number default value of a column, which PostgreSQL returns quoted (ex: \c "'-1'::integer"), compares
      equal to its declared value: schema alignment changed the default value of such a column on every run
    - a column declared with the long name of a type that PostgreSQL returns in a short form (ex:
      \c "double precision", \c "character varying", \c "timestamp with time zone") compares equal to the column
      read from the server: schema alignment changed the type of such a column on every run
    - Added targeted column-migration metadata for resumable offline upgrades, including
      relation identity, dependent objects, and UPDATE trigger functions.
    - bounded maintenance starts with fresh READ COMMITTED snapshots and refuses silently hidden rows,
      while preserving stricter deadlines and restoring session defaults on transaction completion
    - measures text and binary column byte lengths from their uncompressed length metadata,
      without transferring or decompressing large values; ambiguous selections are rejected
    - supports SqlUtil physical-placement updates with a context scoped to the exact live table and
      transaction, including partition movement's source DELETE and destination INSERT trigger calls
    - successful statements clear the context before ordinary writes resume; failed statements preserve
      their original error and require the owning transaction to roll back
    - added explicit non-key update row locking through the SqlUtil @c forupdate_no_key select option;
      independent foreign-key checks can complete while other writers, deletes and key changes remain excluded
    - supports atomic empty-partition removal through SqlUtil; the leaf is detached and dropped in the same
      transaction, so foreign keys that reference the partitioned table remain enforced, and a failed drop leaves
      the leaf attached; parent and leaf locks exclude routed and direct writers, catalog OIDs reject replaced
      relations, and READ COMMITTED visibility prevents stale emptiness proofs; row-level security cannot silently
      hide retained rows
    - discovery exposes runtime parent/leaf OIDs and relation kind in PartitionInfo::driver; these identities
      are not portable schema definitions and must not be reused after a restore or relation replacement
    - nested and foreign partitions are rejected; dependency errors never trigger a cascading drop, and
      successful calls restore caller timeout and row-security settings
    - tables, sequences, and materialized views are listed only from the schemas of the connection's
      \c search_path, as views already were and as on Oracle and MySQL, instead of from every schema in the
      database; an object in <tt>current_schema()</tt> is listed unqualified and one in any other \c search_path
      schema, also a view, as \c "schema.name" (see @ref pgsql_schema_scope)
    - fixed \c listMaterializedViews(), which listed the ordinary tables of every schema instead of materialized
      views
    - preserve partial predicates, INCLUDE columns, NULLS NOT DISTINCT, and per-key sort/null order in
      index introspection, DDL, schema descriptions, and alignment (issue 5470)
    - foreign-key creation now preserves quoted local and referenced column names, including reserved
      words such as \c user and \c order
    - added temporary-table support (\c "create temporary table ... on commit drop"); see
      @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::createTempTable() "AbstractDatabase::createTempTable()"
    - fixed a failed native upsert writing the database error, the SQL statement, and its bind arguments to
      standard output before the exception reached the caller; the statement and arguments are now only added to
      the exception description, and the original error code, exception argument (including the SQLSTATE), and
      exception chain are preserved
    - the UNNEST bulk DML below is used with the native \c pgsql driver only; with JDBC, bulk DML uses batch
      statements
    - \c hasArrayBind() now checks the @ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_HAS_ARRAY_BIND capability flag
      at runtime to detect driver support (requires pgsql driver v3.3+)
    - added UNNEST-based bulk insert SQL generation in \c insertIntern(): when bulk data (hash-of-lists)
      is passed, generates <tt>INSERT INTO ... SELECT ... FROM unnest(...)</tt> instead of row-by-row
      inserts, with element-level type casts to avoid binary array OID conflicts
    - added UNNEST-based bulk upsert via overridden \c getBulkUpsertClosure(): generates
      <tt>INSERT ... SELECT ... FROM unnest(...) ON CONFLICT ... DO UPDATE</tt> for atomic upserts,
      with automatic deduplication of duplicate primary key values within batches
    - added \c PgsqlTable::copyInsert() method for maximum-throughput bulk inserts using the PostgreSQL
      COPY protocol
    - added a \c PgsqlTable::bulkInsert() override using the PostgreSQL COPY protocol for pure
      hash-of-lists input without insert options
    - fixed \c PgsqlTable handling of session temporary tables so unqualified table names and the
      \c pg_temp schema alias resolve to the backend's active temporary schema
    - fixed the resolution of unqualified table and view names, which were always assumed to be in the
      \c public schema; they are now resolved with the connection's \c search_path, as PostgreSQL itself
      resolves them, so table objects can be used in a schema other than \c public without qualifying
      every name (\c listViews() already resolved names this way)
    - fixed the resolution of sequence names, which were matched in any schema of the database rather
      than in the schema the name resolves in; \c getSequence() reported a sequence that does not exist
      on the connection's \c search_path (and then failed reading it, or raised an error when more than
      one schema held the name), and \c listSequences() listed sequences that unqualified SQL on the
      connection cannot reach. Aligning a schema in a database holding more than one schema with the
      same sequence names therefore failed
    - implemented PostgreSQL range partition support for %SqlUtil table creation, introspection, lookup, ensure,
      add, drop, truncate, and detach operations; \c date, \c timestamp, and \c timestamptz partition bounds are
      Qore dates, read from the catalog with the time zone rules that the driver uses for column values of the same
      type, and a bound for a \c date column is the calendar day that contains it in the datasource's time zone
    - added targeted canonical-name partition metadata lookup without loading the complete child-relation catalog
    - added portable list and explicit modulus/remainder hash partitioning, recursive subpartition creation and
      introspection, and catalog-estimated row-count / allocated-size metadata
    - fixed an insert-only upsert, and an upsert of a table whose columns are all key columns, sending \c BEGIN in
      an open transaction and inserting a row that already exists: PostgreSQL wrote a warning and a duplicate key
      error to its log for every such row, every time, as in each schema alignment for its reference data.  With
      PostgreSQL 9.5 or later a row with the same upsert key is now skipped with
      <tt>on conflict (</tt><em>key columns</em><tt>) do nothing</tt>, and \c BEGIN is never sent; a conflict on
      another unique key now raises an exception instead of being skipped

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev18 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.8
    - updated to support \c jdbc and \c odbc drivers as well as the native \c pgsql driver

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev171 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.7.1
    - fixed bugs handling table objects in schemas other than \c public
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4602">issue 4602</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev17 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.7
    - implemented support for generating queries based on generic expressions using the DataProvider module's generic
      expression support
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4538">issue 4538</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev16 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.6
    - added supoport for the \c XML, \c JSON, and \c JSONB types
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4448">issue 4448</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev153 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.5.3
    - fixed handling column names that use reserved words
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4348">issue 4348</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev152 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.5.2
    - added APIs to enable duplicate record creation errors to be detected
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4308">issue 4308</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev151 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.5.1
    - fixed bugs dropping foreign constraints and indices when aligning tables
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4246">issue 4246</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev15 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.5
    - implemented atomic merge support
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3963">issue 3963</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev141 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.4.1
    - fixed a bug generating literal date/time values for SQL queries
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3948">issue 3948</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev14 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.4
    - added support for the DataProvider module
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3545">issue 3545</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev131 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.3.1
    - fixed compatibility with PostgreSQL 12
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3589">issue 3589</a>)
    - fixed comparison of triggers with column restrictions with table alignment
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3466">issue 3466</a>)
    - fixed quoting of reserved words in column names in table alignment
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3400">issue 3400</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev13 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.3
    - added support for serializing and deserializing @ref SqlUtil::AbstractTable "AbstractTable" objects
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/2663">issue 2663</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev121 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.2.1
    - implemented support for custom column operators
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/2314">issue 2314</a>)
    - fixed a bug where default column values were compared incorrectly leading to false positives when comparing and
      aligning DB schemas
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/2527">issue 2527</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev12 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.2
    - updated for complex types

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev112 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.1.2
    - fixed a bug in setting a \c comment for a table column
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/1886">issue 1886</a>)
    - implemented @ref SqlUtil::cop_trunc_date() operator
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/2032">issue 2032</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev111 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.1.1
    - implemented the \a force option (i.e. cascade) for dropping code objects
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/1314">issue 1314</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev11 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.1
    - added support for \c cop_cast operator
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/535">feature 535</a>)
    - added support for the following datatypes and aliases: bool, float, int, timetz, timestamptz, varbit
    - fixed support for the following datatypes and aliases: bit, bit varying, char, character, character varying,
      char varying, oid, varchar
    - fixed bugs handling "bigint" and "double precision" column types with schema alignments
    - fixed a bug with PgsqlTable::tryInsertImpl(); added an explicit "begin" call to make the savepoint work with
      PostgreSQL 9.3+ servers
    - fixed a bug retrieving foreign constraints; columns were not guaranteed to be returned in declaration order
    - implemented support for the \c "returning" clause as an insert option
    - fixed a bug handling tablespaces in unique constraints
    - fixed bugs in @ref SqlUtil::cop_seq() and @ref SqlUtil::cop_seq_currval()
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/624">issue 624</a>)
    - added support for listing PostgreSQL types and materialized views
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/699">issue 699</a>)

    @subsection pgsqlsqlutilbasev10 PgsqlSqlUtilBase v1.0
    - initial release
*/

#! the PgsqlSqlUtilBase namespace contains all the objects in the PgsqlSqlUtilBase module
public namespace PgsqlSqlUtilBase {
#! returns a PgsqlTable object corresponding to the arguments
public PgsqlTable sub get_table(AbstractDatasource nds, string nname, *hash<auto> opts) {
    return new PgsqlTable(nds, nname, opts);
}

#! returns a PgsqlDatabase object corresponding to the arguments
public PgsqlDatabase sub get_database(AbstractDatasource nds, *hash<auto> opts) {
    return new PgsqlDatabase(nds, opts);
}

#! the data type for PostgreSQL NUMBER columns
public class PgsqlNumberDataType inherits SqlUtil::AbstractNumericDataType {
    #! creates the object
    constructor(string native_type, bool nullable, *hash<auto> options) : AbstractNumericDataType(native_type, nullable, options) {
        # cannot bind a string to a numeric column
        base_type_hash -= NT_STRING.toString();
    }
}

#! Returns the schema that resolves unqualified object names on the given connection
/** @param ds the datasource to query

    @return the first existing schema in the connection's \c search_path, or \c "public" if
    \c search_path resolves to no existing schema

    PostgreSQL resolves an unqualified object name against the connection's \c search_path, so the
    schema for such a name is a property of the connection and cannot be assumed to be \c "public".

    @since %PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.9
*/
public string sub get_pgsql_default_schema(AbstractDatasource ds) {
    # current_schema() returns NULL when search_path names no existing schema
    *string schema = ds.selectRow("select current_schema() as \"schema\"").schema;
    return schema ?? "public";
}

# returns an SQL expression giving a relation's name as the connection resolves it: unqualified when the
# relation is in current_schema(), the schema in which PgsqlTable, getView() and getSequence() resolve a
# name without a schema, and "schema.name" otherwise
string sub pgsql_relation_name_expr(string schema_col, string name_col) {
    return sprintf("case when %s = current_schema() then %s else %s || '.' || %s end", schema_col, name_col,
        schema_col, name_col);
}

# returns SQL with the characters outside double-quoted identifiers and single-quoted string literals in lower case,
# as PostgreSQL folds the case of keywords and unquoted identifiers, but not of quoted identifiers or literals
string sub pgsql_lower_unquoted(string sql) {
    string rv = "";
    # in a quoted identifier or literal, a doubled quote is always a quote, so the quantifiers are possessive
    foreach *string part in (regex_extract(sql, "([^\"']++|\"(?:[^\"]|\"\")*+\"?|'(?:[^']|'')*+'?)",
            RE_Global)) {
        if (!part) {
            continue;
        }
        if (part[0] != "\"" && part[0] != "'") {
            part =~ tr/A-Z/a-z/;
        }
        rv += part;
    }
    return rv;
}

# returns the name in the catalog of an identifier: a double-quoted identifier without its quotes and with each
# doubled quote as one quote; an unquoted identifier is returned as given, as unquoted names are looked up as given
string sub pgsql_unquote_identifier(string id) {
    if (id =~ /^"/) {
        return replace(id.substr(1, -1), "\"\"", "\"");
    }
    return id;
}

# returns an identifier for SQL as PostgreSQL's quote_ident() does: as given if it is lower case letters, digits and
# underscores not starting with a digit and not a reserved word, otherwise in double quotes with each quote doubled
string sub pgsql_quote_identifier(string id) {
    if (id =~ /^[a-z_][a-z0-9_]*$/ && !PgsqlDatabase::PgsqlReservedWords{id}) {
        return id;
    }
    return "\"" + replace(id, "\"", "\"\"") + "\"";
}

# returns a name given to SqlUtil as an identifier for SQL: as given if PostgreSQL reads it as one unquoted
# identifier (a letter, an underscore or a non-ASCII character, then letters, digits, underscores, dollar signs or
# non-ASCII characters) that is not a reserved word, so that its letters are folded to lower case as for any
# unquoted name, as before; otherwise in double quotes with each double quote doubled, so that the name can never
# change the SQL that it is part of
string sub pgsql_get_sql_identifier(string name) {
    if (name =~ /\A[A-Za-z_\x{80}-\x{10ffff}][A-Za-z0-9_$\x{80}-\x{10ffff}]*\z/
        && !PgsqlDatabase::PgsqlReservedWords{name.lwr()}) {
        return name;
    }
    return AbstractSqlUtilBase::getDelimitedIdentifier(name, "\"", "\"", "PostgreSQL");
}

# returns True if the argument is one double-quoted identifier, in which each double quote is doubled
bool sub pgsql_is_quoted_identifier(string id) {
    return id =~ /\A"(?:[^"]|"")*+"\z/ ? True : False;
}

# returns a name in the form "name" or "schema.name" for SQL, in which a part given as a double-quoted identifier is
# used as given, and any other part is an identifier as pgsql_get_sql_identifier() returns it
/* the schema is a double-quoted identifier or a name of word characters before the first ".", as
   pgsql_parse_object_name() reads it (ex: \c "\"my.schema\".t" or \c "s.my table")
*/
string sub pgsql_get_sql_object_name(string nname) {
    hash<PgsqlObjectName> on = pgsql_parse_object_name(nname);
    string sql_name = pgsql_is_quoted_identifier(on.sql_name) ? on.sql_name : pgsql_get_sql_identifier(on.sql_name);
    if (!exists on.sql_schema) {
        return sql_name;
    }
    return pgsql_get_sql_schema(on.sql_schema) + "." + sql_name;
}

# returns a schema name as the name of an object gives it, as a double-quoted identifier or a name of word
# characters, for SQL
string sub pgsql_get_sql_schema(string sql_schema) {
    return pgsql_is_quoted_identifier(sql_schema) ? sql_schema : pgsql_get_sql_identifier(sql_schema);
}

# returns a string literal with the name of a relation, as nextval() and currval() take it, which read the name as
# SQL names the relation; a backslash in the name is given in an escape string, which reads it the same way whatever
# the value of standard_conforming_strings
string sub pgsql_get_relation_name_literal(string name) {
    return pgsql_get_string_literal(pgsql_get_sql_object_name(name));
}

# returns a string literal for SQL: in single quotes with each single quote doubled; a string with a backslash is an
# escape string (E'...'), in which each backslash is doubled as well, as PostgreSQL reads a backslash in a string in
# single quotes as an escape character when standard_conforming_strings is off, and an escape string is read the same
# way whatever its value
string sub pgsql_get_string_literal(string v) {
    if (v.find("\\") >= 0) {
        return "E'" + replace(replace(v, "\\", "\\\\"), "'", "''") + "'";
    }
    return "'" + replace(v, "'", "''") + "'";
}

# returns the value of a string literal (ex: 'a''b' or E'a\\b'), or NOTHING if the argument is not a string literal
# with only doubled quotes, and, in an escape string, escaped quotes and backslashes
*string sub pgsql_get_string_literal_value(string sql) {
    *list<*string> l = (sql =~ x/\A'((?:[^']|'')*+)'\z/s);
    if (l) {
        return replace(l[0] ?? "", "''", "'");
    }
    l = (sql =~ x/\A[Ee]'((?:[^'\\]|''|\\['\\])*+)'\z/s);
    if (l) {
        # each doubled quote and each escaped quote or backslash is one character
        string rv = "";
        foreach *string token in (regex_extract(l[0] ?? "", "([^'\\\\]++|''|\\\\['\\\\])", RE_Global)) {
            rv += token.size() == 2 && (token == "''" || token[0] == "\\") ? token[1] : token;
        }
        return rv;
    }
}

# the name of an object in the form "name" or "schema.name", as SQL gives it and as the catalog stores it
hashdecl PgsqlObjectName {
    # the schema in the catalog, if the name has one
    *string schema;
    # the schema as the name gives it, quoted if it is quoted
    *string sql_schema;
    # the name in the catalog
    string name;
    # the name as given, quoted if it is quoted
    string sql_name;
}

# returns the schema and name of an object name in the form "name" or "schema.name"
/* the schema is a double-quoted identifier, which can have any character, "." too, with a quote doubled
   (ex: \c "\"my.schema\".t"), or an unquoted identifier of letters, digits and underscores; the name is the rest of
   the argument after the schema and the ".", and is unquoted only if it is one double-quoted identifier, as a name
   can be followed by an argument list (ex: \c "s.\"F\"(int)").  Unquoted identifiers are not folded to lower case,
   as in previous versions.
*/
hash<PgsqlObjectName> sub pgsql_parse_object_name(string nname) {
    hash<PgsqlObjectName> rv({"name": nname, "sql_name": nname});
    *list<*string> l = (nname =~ x/^("(?:[^"]|"")*+"|\w+)\.((?:"|\w).*)$/s);
    if (l) {
        rv.sql_schema = l[0];
        rv.schema = pgsql_unquote_identifier(l[0]);
        rv.sql_name = rv.name = l[1];
    }
    if (rv.sql_name =~ /^"(?:[^"]|"")*+"$/) {
        rv.name = pgsql_unquote_identifier(rv.sql_name);
    }
    return rv;
}

# matches the action statement of a trigger as PostgreSQL gives it, capturing the function as the trigger names it
# (an unquoted or double-quoted identifier, optionally preceded by a schema as one) and the trigger arguments in
# parentheses (string literals, separated by ", ", in which a quote is doubled)
const PgsqlTriggerExecuteRegex = "^EXECUTE (?:PROCEDURE|FUNCTION) "
    "((?:(?:\\w+|\"(?:[^\"]|\"\")++\")\\.)?(?:\\w+|\"(?:[^\"]|\"\")++\"))"
    "(\\((?:'(?:[^']|'')*+'(?:, *'(?:[^']|'')*+')*+)?\\))$";

# returns the schema, if any, and the name of an SQL name in the form "name" or "schema.name", where each part is an
# identifier, unquoted or double-quoted as PostgreSQL quotes it (ex: \c "\"my schema\".\"F\"\"x\""), or NOTHING if
# the argument is not such a name
*hash<auto> sub pgsql_parse_qualified_name(string sql_name) {
    *list<*string> parts = (sql_name =~ x/^(?:(\w+|"(?:[^"]|"")++")\.)?(\w+|"(?:[^"]|"")++")$/);
    if (!parts) {
        return;
    }
    hash<auto> rv = {"name": pgsql_unquote_identifier(parts[1])};
    if (parts[0]) {
        rv.schema = pgsql_unquote_identifier(parts[0]);
    }
    return rv;
}

#! PostgreSQL savepoint helper
public class PgsqlSavepointHelper inherits AbstractSavepointHelper {
    #! Creates the object
    constructor(AbstractDatasource ds, *string savepoint) : AbstractSavepointHelper(ds, savepoint) {
    }

    #! Creates the savepoint
    private createSavepointImpl() {
        ds.exec("savepoint " + pgsql_get_sql_identifier(savepoint));
    }

    #! Rolls back to the savepoint
    private rollbackImpl() {
        ds.exec("rollback to savepoint " + pgsql_get_sql_identifier(savepoint));
    }

    #! Deletes the savepoint
    private deleteSavepointImpl() {
        ds.exec("release savepoint " + pgsql_get_sql_identifier(savepoint));
    }
}

#! represents a PostgreSQL type
public class PgsqlType inherits Qore::Serializable {
    public {
        #! the name of the type
        string name;

        #! the source of the type
        string src;
    }

    #! creates the type from the supplied arguments
    constructor(string n_name, string n_src) {
        name = n_name;
        src = n_src;
    }

    list<hash<auto>> getTypeList() {
        list<string> sl = src.split(",");
        trim sl;

        list<hash<auto>> l = ();
        foreach string str in (sl) {
            (*string tname, *string type) = (str =~ x/(\w+) (.*)/);
            if (!tname || !type) {
                throw "TYPE-COMPONENT-ERROR", sprintf("type %y: can't find element name and type in element "
                    "%d %y of source %y", name, $# + 1, str, src);
            }
            l += {
                "name": tname,
                "type": type,
            };
        }
        return l;
    }

    list<string> getModifySql(PgsqlType old, *hash opt) {
        list<string> sqll = ();

        list tl = getTypeList();
        list oldtl = old.getTypeList();

        #printf("tl: %Y\noldtl: %Y\n", tl, oldtl);

        # process differences in old attributes
        foreach hash oldh in (oldtl.iterator()) {
            if ($# >= tl.size()) {
                sqll += sprintf("alter type %s drop attribute %s", name, oldh.name);
                continue;
            }
            hash h = tl[$#];
            if (oldh.type != h.type)
                sqll += sprintf("alter type %s alter attribute %s type %s%s", name, oldh.name, h.type,
                    opt.force ? " cascade" : "");
            if (oldh.name != h.name)
                sqll += sprintf("alter type %s rename attribute %s to %s", name, oldh.name, h.name);
        }
        # add any new attributes
        for (int i = oldtl.size(); i < tl.size(); ++i)
            sqll += sprintf("alter type %s add attribute %s %s", name, tl[i].name, tl[i].type);

        return sqll;
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to create the type in the database
    string getCreateSql(*hash opt) {
        return sprintf("create type %s as (%s)", name, src);
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to drop the function from the database
    /** @param opt drop options; if \a force is @ref True "True", then the \c CASCADE option will be used in the drop statement
        */
    string getDropSql(*hash opt) {
        return sprintf("drop type %s%s", name, opt.force ? " cascade" : "");
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to rename a type
    /** @param new_name the new name of the object
        @param opt this argument is ignored
        */
    string getRenameSql(string new_name, *hash opt) {
        return sprintf("alter type %s rename to %s", name, new_name);
    }

    #! returns @ref True if the types are equal
    bool equal(PgsqlType type) {
        return name == type.name && src == type.src;
    }
}

#! represents a PostgreSQL-specific index
public class PgsqlIndex inherits SqlUtil::AbstractIndex {
    public {
        #! the tablespace name of the index
        *string tablespace;

        #! access method for the index (e.g. \c "btree", \c "hash", \c "gist", \c "gin", \c "brin",
        #! \c "ivfflat", \c "hnsw"); \c NOTHING means the driver default (btree)
        *string method;

        #! method-specific storage parameters; rendered as \c "WITH (k1 = v1, ...)" in the CREATE INDEX
        *hash<auto> with_options;

        #! per-column operator class hash (column name → opclass name); PostgreSQL supports attaching an
        #! operator class to each indexed column (e.g. \c {"embedding": "vector_cosine_ops"} for an ivfflat
        #! index over a pgvector column)
        *hash<string, string> opclass;

        #! SQL predicate restricting the rows indexed; NOTHING for a full index
        *string predicate;

        #! Non-key covering columns, in storage order (PostgreSQL 11+)
        list<string> include;

        #! Per-key sort direction: asc or desc; omitted keys default to asc
        hash<string, string> order;

        #! Per-key null placement: first or last; defaults to last for asc and first for desc
        hash<string, string> nulls;

        #! Treat NULL keys as equal in a unique index (PostgreSQL 15+)
        bool nulls_not_distinct;
    }

    #! Creates a PostgreSQL index definition
    /** @param n_name index name
        @param n_unique whether the index enforces uniqueness
        @param n_cols ordered key names and column descriptions
        @param n_tablespace optional index tablespace
        @param n_method optional access method; defaults to btree
        @param n_with optional storage parameters
        @param n_opclass optional per-key operator classes
        @param options additional options: where, include, nulls_not_distinct, order, and nulls
        @throw INDEX-OPTION-ERROR for invalid key modifiers or incompatible options
        @note INCLUDE columns are stored separately from the uniqueness keys.
        @see pgsql_index_options for a fulfillment index example
    */
    constructor(string n_name, bool n_unique, hash n_cols, *string n_tablespace,
            *string n_method, *hash<auto> n_with, *hash<string, string> n_opclass, *hash<auto> options)
            : AbstractIndex(n_name, n_unique, n_cols) {
        tablespace = n_tablespace;
        method = n_method;
        with_options = n_with;
        opclass = n_opclass;
        predicate = options.where;
        if (exists predicate && predicate =~ /^\s*$/) {
            throw "INDEX-OPTION-ERROR", "An index predicate must not be empty";
        }
        include = options.include ?? ();
        order = options.order ?? {};
        nulls = options.nulls ?? {};
        nulls_not_distinct = options.nulls_not_distinct ?? False;
        if (nulls_not_distinct && !unique) {
            throw "INDEX-OPTION-ERROR", "NULLS NOT DISTINCT requires a unique index";
        }
        foreach string col in (order.keys()) {
            order{col} = order{col}.lwr();
            if (!columns.hasKey(col) || (order{col} != "asc" && order{col} != "desc")) {
                throw "INDEX-OPTION-ERROR", sprintf("Invalid index sort direction for %y", col);
            }
        }
        foreach string col in (nulls.keys()) {
            nulls{col} = nulls{col}.lwr();
            if (!columns.hasKey(col) || (nulls{col} != "first" && nulls{col} != "last")) {
                throw "INDEX-OPTION-ERROR", sprintf("Invalid index null placement for %y", col);
            }
        }
        if ((order || nulls) && method && method.lwr() != "btree") {
            throw "INDEX-OPTION-ERROR", "Index ordering requires the btree access method";
        }
        hash<string, bool> seen;
        foreach string col in (include) {
            if (!col || columns.hasKey(col) || seen{col}) {
                throw "INDEX-OPTION-ERROR", sprintf("Invalid or duplicate INCLUDE column %y", col);
            }
            seen{col} = True;
        }
    }

    #! Returns CREATE INDEX SQL preserving all recorded index options
    /** @param table_name qualified SQL table name
        @param opt optional index_tablespace override
        @return the CREATE INDEX statement
        @see pgsql_index_options for a fulfillment index example
    */
    string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash opt) {
        # build column list with optional per-column operator class; follow the same
        # key → ddl_name resolution as Columns::getSqlColumnNameIterator() so reserved-word
        # columns are still correctly quoted
        string cols_sql;
        {
            list<string> parts = ();
            HashIterator col_it = columns.iterator();
            while (col_it.next()) {
                string cn = col_it.getKey();
                auto c = col_it.getValue();
                string name_sql;
                if (c.typeCode() == NT_OBJECT && c instanceof AbstractColumn) {
                    AbstractColumn ac = c;
                    name_sql = ac.ddl_name.val() ? ac.ddl_name : cn;
                } else if (c.typeCode() == NT_HASH && c.ddl_name.val()) {
                    name_sql = c.ddl_name;
                } else {
                    # an index "column" that is not a column of the table is an SQL expression, as it is for
                    # every database (see AbstractTable::addIndex())
                    name_sql = cn;
                }
                if (opclass{cn}.val()) {
                    name_sql += " " + opclass{cn};
                }
                if (order{cn}) {
                    name_sql += " " + order{cn};
                }
                if (nulls{cn}) {
                    name_sql += " nulls " + nulls{cn};
                }
                parts += name_sql;
            }
            cols_sql = parts.join(", ");
        }

        string sql = sprintf("create %sindex %s on %s", unique ? "unique " : "", pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name),
            table_name);
        if (method.val()) {
            sql += sprintf(" using %s", method);
        }
        sql += sprintf(" (%s)", cols_sql);
        if (include) {
            # INCLUDE accepts identifiers, never SQL expressions. Always quote and escape them.
            list<string> quoted;
            foreach string col in (include) {
                quoted += AbstractSqlUtilBase::getDelimitedIdentifier(col, "\"", "\"", "PostgreSQL");
            }
            sql += " include (" + quoted.join(", ") + ")";
        }
        if (nulls_not_distinct) {
            sql += " nulls not distinct";
        }

        if (with_options) {
            list<string> parts = ();
            HashIterator wi(with_options);
            while (wi.next()) {
                parts += sprintf("%s = %s", wi.getKey(), wi.getValue());
            }
            sql += sprintf(" with (%s)", parts.join(", "));
        }

        *string ts = opt.index_tablespace ? opt.index_tablespace : tablespace;
        if (ts) {
            sql += sprintf(" tablespace %s", pgsql_get_sql_identifier(ts));
        }
        if (predicate) {
            sql += " where " + predicate;
        }
        return sql;
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to drop the index from the database
    string getDropSql(string table_name) {
        return sprintf("drop index if exists %s", pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name));
    }

    #! returns @ref True "True" if the argument is equal to the current index, @ref False "False" if not
    private bool equalImpl(AbstractIndex ix) {
        PgsqlIndex pix = cast<PgsqlIndex>(ix);
        return predicate == pix.predicate && equalFeatures(pix);
    }

    private bool equalFeatures(PgsqlIndex pix) {
        if (include != pix.include || nulls_not_distinct != pix.nulls_not_distinct) {
            return False;
        }
        foreach string col in (columns.keys()) {
            string direction = order{col} ?? "asc";
            string other_direction = pix.order{col} ?? "asc";
            if (direction != other_direction
                    || (nulls{col} ?? (direction == "desc" ? "first" : "last"))
                    != (pix.nulls{col} ?? (other_direction == "desc" ? "first" : "last"))) {
                return False;
            }
        }
        # normalize method comparison: NOTHING and "btree" are the PostgreSQL default
        string m1 = method.val() ? method.lwr() : "btree";
        string m2 = pix.method.val() ? pix.method.lwr() : "btree";
        if (m1 != m2) {
            return False;
        }
        # hash equality is order-sensitive in Qore, but storage options and opclasses are
        # semantically order-independent, so compare key-by-key
        if (!AbstractIndex::hashesEqualUnordered(with_options, pix.with_options)) {
            return False;
        }
        if (!AbstractIndex::hashesEqualUnordered(opclass, pix.opclass)) {
            return False;
        }
        return True;
    }

    #! Compares index definitions using PostgreSQL's parsed predicate representation
    /** @param ix the index to compare
        @param ds datasource containing the indexed table
        @param table_name qualified SQL table name
        @return True if the keys, options, and parsed predicates match
        @throw DBI-ERROR if PostgreSQL cannot plan a predicate
        @note Uses EXPLAIN without ANALYZE; no table rows are read or modified. The caller must hold
        the table lock and ensure that all referenced columns already exist.

        @par Example
        Schema alignment compares a declared active-order predicate with PostgreSQL's stored expression,
        including any casts inserted by the server.
    */
    bool equalForAlignment(AbstractIndex ix, AbstractDatasource ds, string table_name) {
        if (!(ix instanceof PgsqlIndex)) {
            return False;
        }
        PgsqlIndex pix = cast<PgsqlIndex>(ix);
        if (name != pix.name || unique != pix.unique || columns.keys() != pix.columns.keys()
                || !equalFeatures(pix)) {
            return False;
        }
        if (predicate == pix.predicate) {
            return True;
        }
        if (!predicate || !pix.predicate) {
            return False;
        }
        return predicateOutput(ds, table_name, predicate) == predicateOutput(ds, table_name, pix.predicate);
    }

    private static string predicateOutput(AbstractDatasource ds, string table_name, string predicate) {
        hash<auto> row = ds.selectRow("explain (verbose, costs off, format json) select (" + predicate
            + ") from " + table_name);
        auto plan = row{"QUERY PLAN"};
        if (plan.typeCode() == NT_STRING) {
            plan = parse_json(plan);
        }
        *string output = plan[0].Plan.Output[0];
        if (!output) {
            throw "INDEX-DEFINITION-ERROR", "PostgreSQL did not return a parsed index predicate";
        }
        return output;
    }

    #! returns the SQL statement that renames the index in the database
    softlist<string> getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
        return sprintf("alter index %s rename to %s", pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name),
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(new_name));
    }

}

#! represents a PostgreSQL-specific foreign constraint
public class PgsqlForeignConstraint inherits SqlUtil::AbstractForeignConstraint {
    #! creates the constraint from the supplied arguments
    constructor(string n, Columns c, ForeignConstraintTarget t, bool deferrable = False,
            bool initially_deferred = False, *string on_delete, *string on_update)
            : AbstractForeignConstraint(n, c, t, deferrable, initially_deferred, on_delete, on_update) {
    }

    #! maps a pg_constraint confdeltype/confupdtype code to a referential action, or NOTHING for the default
    /** @param code the single-character code from \c pg_constraint.confdeltype or \c confupdtype

        @return the referential action (see @ref fk_actions), or NOTHING for the database default (\c "a", no
        action)

        @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.19
    */
    static *string actionFromCatalog(string code) {
        switch (code) {
            case "a": return;              # no action (the default)
            case "r": return FK_RESTRICT;
            case "c": return FK_CASCADE;
            case "n": return FK_SET_NULL;
            case "d": return FK_SET_DEFAULT;
        }
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to create the constraint in the database
    string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash opt) {
        return getCreateSql(name, table_name, opt);
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to drop the constraint from the database
    string getDropSql(string table_name) {
        return sprintf("alter table %s drop constraint if exists %s cascade", table_name,
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name));
    }

    #! returns the SQL that renames the constraint in place
    softlist getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
        return sprintf("alter table %s rename constraint %s to %s", table_name,
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name), pgsql_get_sql_identifier(new_name));
    }

    #! Returns foreign-key DDL with the recorded SQL names of all participating columns
    /** @param name constraint name
        @param table_name SQL name of the referencing table
        @param opt optional SQL generation options
        @return an ALTER TABLE statement preserving column quoting, order, and deferrability

        @par Example
        @code{.sql}
alter table draft_guard add constraint fk_draft foreign key (draft_user) references drafts ("user")
        @endcode
    */
    string getCreateSql(string name, string table_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
        return sprintf("alter table %s add constraint %s foreign key (%s) references %s (%s)%s%s", table_name,
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name), (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, getSqlColumnNameIterator()),
            pgsql_get_sql_object_name(target.table),
            (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, target.columns.getSqlColumnNameIterator()),
            getActionsSql(), getDeferrabilitySql());
    }
}

#! represents a PostgreSQL-specific check constraint
public class PgsqlCheckConstraint inherits SqlUtil::AbstractCheckConstraint {
    #! creates the constraint from the supplied arguments
    constructor(string n, string n_src) : AbstractCheckConstraint(n, n_src) {
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to drop the constraint from the database
    string getDropSql(string table_name) {
        return sprintf("alter table %s drop constraint %s cascade", table_name,
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name));
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to create the constraint in the database
    string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash opt) {
        return getCreateSql(name, table_name, opt);
    }

    #! returns the SQL that renames the constraint in place
    list getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
        return (sprintf("alter table %s rename constraint %s to %s", table_name,
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name), pgsql_get_sql_identifier(new_name)),);
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to create the constraint in the database
    string getCreateSql(string name, string table_name, *hash opt) {
        return sprintf("alter table %s add constraint %s check (%s)", table_name, pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name),
            src);
    }
}

#! represents a PostgreSQL-specific column
public class PgsqlColumn inherits SqlUtil::AbstractColumn {
    public {
        #! byte size of the column
        int byte_size;

        #! A number constant read from the server: its value without the quotes of a negative number, and its type
        const NumberConstantRegex = "^'?([^':]*)'?::(smallint|integer|bigint|numeric|real|double precision)$";

        #! A number literal in SQL
        const NumberLiteralRegex = "^[-+]?([0-9]+(\\.[0-9]*)?|\\.[0-9]+)([eE][-+]?[0-9]+)?$";
    }

    #! empty constructor for subclasses
    private constructor() {
    }

    #! creates the column from the supplied arguments
    constructor(string n, string nt, *string qt, int sz, bool nul, *string dv, *string cm, *softint bs, *int scale)
            : AbstractColumn(n, nt, qt, sz, nul, dv, cm, scale) {
        if (!exists bs) { bs = 0; }
        byte_size = bs;
    }

    #! returns a string giving the native type of the column
    string getNativeTypeString() {
        switch (native_type) {
            case =~ /^timestamptz/: {
                string sql = native_type;
                splice sql, 11, 0, "(" + string(size) + ")";
                return sql;
            }
            case =~ /^timestamp/: {
                string sql = native_type;
                splice sql, 9, 0, "(" + string(size) + ")";
                return sql;
            }
            case =~ /^timetz/: {
                string sql = native_type;
                splice sql, 6, 0, "(" + string(size) + ")";
                return sql;
            }
            case =~ /^time/: {
                string sql = native_type;
                splice sql, 4, 0, "(" + string(size) + ")";
                return sql;
            }
        }
        return AbstractColumn::getNativeTypeString();
    }

    #! returns a list of sql strings that can be used to add the column to an existing table
    /** @par Example:
        @code{.py}
list l = col.getAddColumnSql(t);
        @endcode

        @param t the SqlUtil::AbstractTable object to modify
        */
    list getAddColumnSql(AbstractTable t) {
        list l += sprintf("alter table %s add %s", t.getSqlName(), AbstractColumn::getCreateSql(t));
        if (comment)
            l += sprintf("comment on column %s.%s is %s", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name, t.getSqlValue(comment));
        return l;
    }

    #! returns a list of sql strings that can be used to modify the column to the new definition
    /** if the column definitions are identical then an empty list is returned

        @par Example:
        @code{.py}
list l = col.getModifySql(t, newcol);
        @endcode

        The column names are assumed to be equal.

        @param t the SqlUtil::AbstractTable object to modify
        @param col the new column definition
        @param opt column modification options (none are supported by this class)

        @return a list of sql strings that can be used to modify the column to the new definition; if the column
        definitions are identical then an empty list is returned
        */
    list getModifySqlImpl(AbstractTable t, AbstractColumn col, *hash opt) {
        list l = ();
        PgsqlColumn c = col;

        # get new native type declaration string
        string nts = c.getNativeTypeString();
        if (getNativeTypeString() != nts) {
            #printf("ME: %s\nDB: %s\n", nts, getNativeTypeString());
            # first drop any default value if it's changing
            if (def_val !== c.def_val)
                l += sprintf("alter table %s alter %s drop default", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name);

            l += sprintf("alter table %s alter %s type %s", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name, nts);

            if (def_val !== c.def_val && exists c.def_val && c.def_val !== NULL)
                l += sprintf("alter table %s alter %s set default %s", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name, c.def_val);
        } else if (def_val !== c.def_val) {
            # issue #2527: ensure that mismatches are not due to server type info
            if (!PgsqlColumn::equalDefaultValue(def_val, c.def_val)) {
                #printf("L: %Y\nXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\nR: %Y\n\n", self, c);
                if (exists c.def_val && c.def_val !== NULL)
                    l += sprintf("alter table %s alter %s set default %s", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name, c.def_val);
                else
                    l += sprintf("alter table %s alter %s drop default", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name);
            }
        }
        if (nullable != c.nullable) {
            l += sprintf("alter table %s alter %s %s not null", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name,
                c.nullable ? "drop" : "set");
        }

        if (c.comment != comment)
            l += sprintf("comment on column %s.%s is %s", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name, t.getSqlValue(c.comment));
        return l;
    }

    #! Returns @ref True "True" if a default value read from the server is the declared default value
    /** PostgreSQL returns a default value constant with the type of the constant (ex: \c "'abc'::character varying"
        for \c 'abc'), and a negative number constant quoted (ex: \c "'-1'::integer" for \c -1,
        \c "'-1.5'::numeric" for \c -1.5, also on a column of another numeric type); the quotes of a number constant
        are not part of its value, whereas the quotes of a string constant are, so a value whose constant has a string
        type is compared as it is

        @param server_val the default value read from the server
        @param declared_val the declared default value in SQL

        @return @ref True "True" if the values are the same

        @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.16
    */
    static bool equalDefaultValue(*string server_val, *string declared_val) {
        if (server_val === declared_val) {
            return True;
        }
        # a string constant: compared by its value, so that a declared escape string (ex: E'a\\b', as
        # getSqlValue() gives a string with a backslash) is the constant that the server returns (ex: 'a\b'::text, or
        # E'a\\b'::text when standard_conforming_strings is off)
        if (declared_val.val() && server_val.val()) {
            *string declared_value = pgsql_get_string_literal_value(declared_val);
            if (exists declared_value) {
                string sv = server_val;
                sv =~ s/::[^']*$//;
                *string server_value = pgsql_get_string_literal_value(sv);
                if (exists server_value && server_value == declared_value) {
                    return True;
                }
            }
        }
        if (!declared_val.val() || declared_val =~ /::/ || server_val !~ /::/) {
            return False;
        }
        string ndv = server_val;
        ndv =~ s/::.*//;
        if (ndv == declared_val) {
            return True;
        }
        # a number constant: compared by its value, so that a declared -1.5 is the returned '-1.50'
        *list<*string> num = regex_extract(server_val, PgsqlColumn::NumberConstantRegex);
        return num && regex(declared_val, PgsqlColumn::NumberLiteralRegex)
            && regex(num[0], PgsqlColumn::NumberLiteralRegex) && number(num[0]) == number(declared_val);
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to rename the column
    /** @par Example:
        @code{.py}
string str = col.getRenameSql(t, "new_name");
        @endcode

        @param t the SqlUtil::AbstractTable object to modify
        @param new_name the new name for the column

        @return a string that can be used to rename the column
    */
    string getRenameSql(AbstractTable t, string new_name) {
        return sprintf("alter table %s rename %s to %s", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name, getDdlName(new_name));
    }

    #! returns the column name with quoting in case the column name is a reserved word
    /** the name returned here will be used when executing DDL
    */
    string getDdlName(string name) {
        return pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name);
    }

    #! returns @ref True "True" if the argument is equal to the current object, @ref False "False" if not
    private bool equalImpl(AbstractColumn c) {
        return True;
    }
}

#! represents a PostgreSQL-specific numeric column
public class PgsqlNumericColumn inherits PgsqlColumn {
    #! creates the column from the supplied arguments
    constructor(string n, string nt, *string qt, int sz, bool nul, *string dv, *string cm, *softint bs, *softint scale)
            : PgsqlColumn(n, nt, qt, sz, nul, dv, cm, bs, scale) {
    }

    #! returns a string giving the native type of the column
    string getNativeTypeString() {
        switch (native_type) {
            # do not return with numeric precision
            case "bigint":
            case "double precision":
                return native_type;
        }
        return PgsqlColumn::getNativeTypeString();
    }
}

#! common base class for unique constraints
public class PgsqlUniqueConstraintCommon inherits SqlUtil::AbstractUniqueConstraint {
    private {
        #! any tablespace for the unique key index
        *string tablespace;
    }

    #! creates the constraint with an optional tablespace name
    constructor(*string ts) {
        tablespace = ts;
    }

    #! returns the tablespace name used for this constraint, if known
    *string getTablespace() {
        return tablespace;
    }

    #! sets or clears the tablespace name
    setTablespace(*string ts) {
        tablespace = ts;
    }

    #! sets the supporting index name
    bool setIndexBase(string ix) {
        if (index && index != ix)
            return False;
        index = ix;
        delete tablespace;
        return True;
    }

    #! clears any index base for the constraint
    clearIndex() {
        delete index;
    }

    #! adds index options onto the sql creation string
    getIndexSql(reference<string> sql, string name, *hash opts) {
        *string ix = opts.index ? opts.index : index;
        if (ix) {
            sql += sprintf(" using index %s", pgsql_get_sql_identifier(ix));
        } else {
            sql += sprintf(" (%s)", (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, getSqlColumnNameIterator()));

            *string ts = opts.index_tablespace ? opts.index_tablespace : tablespace;
            if (ts) {
                sql += sprintf(" using index tablespace %s", pgsql_get_sql_identifier(ts));
            }
        }
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to drop the constraint from the database
    string getDropSql(string table_name) {
        return sprintf("alter table %s drop constraint %s cascade", table_name,
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name));
    }
}

#! class modeling a unique constraint
public class PgsqlUniqueConstraint inherits SqlUtil::AbstractUniqueConstraint, PgsqlUniqueConstraintCommon {
    private {
        #! @ref True "True" if the constraint is enabled, @ref False "False" if not
        bool enabled;
    }

    #! creates the object with the given attributes
    /** @param n name of the constraint
        @param n_cols column hash
        @param e the enabled status of the constraint
        @param ts optional tablespace name
    */
    constructor(string n, hash n_cols, bool e = True, *string ts)
            : AbstractUniqueConstraint(n, n_cols), PgsqlUniqueConstraintCommon(ts) {
        enabled = e;
    }

    #! returns the PgsqlColumn value of the given key if it exists, otherwise throws a \c KEY-ERROR exception
    /** @par Example:
        @code{.py}
auto v = c.name;
        @endcode

        @param k the name of the key to access

        @return the value of the given key in the contained hash if it exists

        @throw KEY-ERROR the given key does not exist in the contained hash

        @note this method is called automatically when an unknown or inaccessible member name is accessed from outside the class

        @see @ref memberGate_methods
    */
    PgsqlColumn memberGate(string k) {
        if (!h.hasKey(k)) {
            throw "KEY-ERROR", sprintf("cannot access unknown %s %y; expecting one of: %y", getElementName(), k,
                h.keys());
        }
        return h{k};
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to create the constraint
    string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash opts) {
        return getCreateSql(name, table_name, opts);
    }

    #! returns the SQL that renames the constraint, and its index with it
    /** The constraint is renamed in place: dropping it and adding it under the new name would drop the foreign
        constraints that reference it, as its drop cascades

        @param table_name the name of the table
        @param new_name the new name of the constraint

        @return the \c ALTER \c TABLE ... \c RENAME \c CONSTRAINT statement in a list
    */
    list<auto> getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
        return (sprintf("alter table %s rename constraint %s to %s", table_name,
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name), pgsql_get_sql_identifier(new_name)),);
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to rename the constraint
    string getCreateSql(string name, string table_name, *hash<auto> opts) {
        string sql = sprintf("alter table %s add constraint %s unique ", table_name, pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name));
        getIndexSql(\sql, name, opts);
        return sql;
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to drop the constraint from the database
    string getDropSql(string table_name) {
        return sprintf("alter table %s drop constraint %s cascade", table_name,
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name));
    }

    #! returns @ref True "True" if the constraint is enabled, @ref False "False" if not
    bool isEnabled() {
        return enabled;
    }
}

#! represents a PostgreSQL-specific primary key constraint
public class PgsqlPrimaryKey inherits SqlUtil::AbstractPrimaryKey, PgsqlUniqueConstraintCommon {
    #! creates an empty primary key object
    constructor() {
    }

    #! creates the object with the given attributes
    /** @param n name of the constraint
        @param c column hash
        @param ts optional tablespace name
    */
    constructor(string n, *hash c, *string ts) : AbstractPrimaryKey(n, c), PgsqlUniqueConstraintCommon(ts) {
    }

    #! returns the PgsqlColumn value of the given key if it exists, otherwise throws a \c KEY-ERROR exception
    /** @par Example:
        @code{.py}
auto v = c.name;
        @endcode

        @param k the name of the key to access

        @return the value of the given key in the contained hash if it exists

        @throw KEY-ERROR the given key does not exist in the contained hash

        @note this method is called automatically when an unknown or inaccessible member name is accessed from outside the class

        @see @ref memberGate_methods
    */
    PgsqlColumn memberGate(string k) {
        if (!h.hasKey(k))
            throw "KEY-ERROR", sprintf("cannot access unknown %s %y; expecting one of: %y", getElementName(), k,
                h.keys());
        return h{k};
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to create the constraint
    string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash<auto> opts) {
        string sql = sprintf("alter table %s add constraint %s primary key", table_name,
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name));
        getIndexSql(\sql, name, opts);
        return sql;
    }

    #! returns the SQL that renames the constraint, and its index with it
    softlist getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
        return sprintf("alter index %s rename to %s", pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name),
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(new_name));
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to drop the constraint from the database
    string getDropSql(string table_name) {
        return sprintf("alter table %s drop constraint %s cascade", table_name,
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name));
    }
}

#! class for PostgreSQL sequences
public class PgsqlSequence inherits SqlUtil::AbstractSequence {
    public {
        #! the minimum value; no value for the default minimum (1 for an ascending sequence, the minimum of a
        #! bigint for a descending one)
        /** @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.19
        */
        *number min;

        #! @ref True if the sequence starts again at its minimum (or maximum) value when it reaches its maximum (or
        #! minimum) value
        /** @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.19
        */
        bool cycle = False;
    }

    #! creates the object from the arguments
    /** @param n_name the name of the sequence
        @param n_start the starting value
        @param n_increment the increment
        @param n_end the maximum value; no value for the default maximum (the maximum of a bigint for an ascending
        sequence, -1 for a descending one)
        @param n_min the minimum value; no value for the default minimum
        @param n_cycle @ref True if the sequence starts again when it reaches its limit

        @note \a n_min and \a n_cycle are available since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.19
    */
    constructor(string n_name, number n_start = 1, number n_increment = 1, *softnumber n_end, *softnumber n_min,
            bool n_cycle = False) : AbstractSequence(n_name, n_start, n_increment, n_end) {
        min = n_min;
        cycle = n_cycle;
    }

    #! applies a sequence description, setting the minimum and cycle option that PostgreSQL supports
    /** @param desc the sequence description (see
            @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::SequenceDescriptionOptions "SequenceDescriptionOptions")

        @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.19
    */
    setDescription(hash<auto> desc) {
        if (desc.hasKey("min")) {
            min = desc.min;
        }
        if (desc.hasKey("cycle")) {
            cycle = desc.cycle.toBool();
        }
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to create the sequence in the database
    string getCreateSql(*hash opt) {
        string sql = sprintf("create sequence %s start with %d increment by %d ", pgsql_get_sql_object_name(name),
            start, increment);
        sql += exists max ? sprintf("maxvalue %d", max) : "no maxvalue";
        if (exists min) {
            sql += sprintf(" minvalue %d", min);
        }
        if (cycle) {
            sql += " cycle";
        }
        return sql;
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to rename the sequence in the database
    /** The object is renamed in place with this call
        @param new_name the new name of the object
        @param opt this option is ignored
        */
    softlist getRenameSql(string new_name, *hash opt) {
        return sprintf("alter sequence %s rename to %s", pgsql_get_sql_object_name(name),
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(new_name));
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to drop the sequence from the database
    /** @param opt this argument is ignored

        @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.19 the name of the sequence is quoted if it is not an ordinary name
    */
    string getDropSql(*hash<auto> opt) {
        return sprintf("drop sequence %s", pgsql_get_sql_object_name(name));
    }

    #! returns True if two optional numeric values differ, treating a present 0 as a value, not as no value
    /** @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.19
    */
    static bool valueDiffers(*number a, *number b) {
        if (exists a != exists b) {
            return True;
        }
        return exists a && a != b;
    }

    #! returns the SQL that alters the sequence to match the description, preserving its current value
    /** The increment, minimum, maximum and cycle option are aligned with \c ALTER \c SEQUENCE; the starting value
        is not changed, as \c ALTER \c SEQUENCE \c RESTART would reset the current value, and \c START \c WITH only
        changes the value that a later \c RESTART uses.  PostgreSQL has no sequence cache in the SqlUtil model, so
        the \c cache option is ignored.

        @param desired the sequence description (see
            @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::SequenceDescriptionOptions "SequenceDescriptionOptions")
        @param opt this argument is ignored

        @return the \c ALTER \c SEQUENCE statement for the parameters that differ, or NOTHING if none differ

        @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.19
    */
    *softlist<string> getAlterSql(hash<auto> desired, *hash<auto> opt) {
        list<string> clauses = ();
        if (exists desired.increment && desired.increment != increment) {
            clauses += sprintf("increment by %d", desired.increment);
        }
        # the direction of the sequence after the alignment, for the default of a maximum or minimum
        bool ascending = (exists desired.increment ? desired.increment : increment) > 0;
        # the maximum: "max" or the legacy "end"; a value equal to the bigint default in the direction, or
        # NOTHING, means the database default (no maximum), as getSequenceImpl() reports it, so neither alters
        if (desired.hasKey("max") || desired.hasKey("end")) {
            *int new_max = desired.hasKey("max") ? desired.max : desired.end;
            if (exists new_max && new_max == (ascending ? 9223372036854775807 : -1)) {
                remove new_max;
            }
            # compare and render with "exists", as a maximum of 0 is a valid explicit value (a descending
            # sequence can have "maxvalue 0") that .val() would wrongly treat as no value
            if (PgsqlSequence::valueDiffers(new_max, max)) {
                clauses += exists new_max ? sprintf("maxvalue %d", new_max) : "no maxvalue";
            }
        }
        if (desired.hasKey("min")) {
            *int new_min = desired.min;
            if (exists new_min && new_min == (ascending ? 1 : -9223372036854775807 - 1)) {
                remove new_min;
            }
            if (PgsqlSequence::valueDiffers(new_min, min)) {
                clauses += exists new_min ? sprintf("minvalue %d", new_min) : "no minvalue";
            }
        }
        if (desired.hasKey("cycle")) {
            bool new_cycle = desired.cycle.toBool();
            if (new_cycle != cycle) {
                clauses += new_cycle ? "cycle" : "no cycle";
            }
        }
        if (!clauses) {
            return;
        }
        return sprintf("alter sequence %s %s", pgsql_get_sql_object_name(name), clauses.join(" "));
    }
}

#! represents a PostgreSQL view
public class PgsqlView inherits SqlUtil::AbstractView {
    public {
        #! Owner of the view
        *string viewowner;
    }

    #! creates the object from the arguments
    constructor(string n_name, string n_src, *string n_schemaname,
                *string n_viewowner)
            : AbstractView(n_name, n_src) {
        schema = n_schemaname;
        viewowner = n_viewowner;
        updatable = False;
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to create the view in the database
    string getCreateSql(*hash opt) {
        # TODO/FIXME: more pgsql options - what's allows in "opt"?
        return sprintf("create %s%sview %s as %s",
            opt.replace ? "or replace " : "",
            opt.temp ? "temp " : "",
            pgsql_get_sql_object_name(name),
            src
        );
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to rename the view in the database
    /** The object is renamed in place with this call
        @param new_name the new name of the object
        @param opt this option is ignored
        */
    softlist getRenameSql(string new_name, *hash opt) {
        return sprintf("alter view %s rename to %s", pgsql_get_sql_object_name(name),
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(new_name));
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to drop the view from the database
    /** @param opt this argument is ignored

        @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.19 the name of the view is quoted if it is not an ordinary name
    */
    string getDropSql(*hash<auto> opt) {
        return sprintf("drop view %s", pgsql_get_sql_object_name(name));
    }
}

#! represents a PostgreSQL-specific trigger
public class PgsqlTrigger inherits SqlUtil::AbstractTrigger {
    #! creates the object and sets its name and the trigger source
    constructor(string n, string n_src) : AbstractTrigger(n, n_src) {
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to create the trigger in the database
    softlist<string> getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash opt) {
        return sprintf("create trigger %s %s", pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name), src);
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to drop the trigger from the database
    softlist<string> getDropSql(string table_name) {
        return sprintf("drop trigger %s on %s", pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name), table_name);
    }

    #! returns @ref True "True" if the argument is equal to the current object, @ref False "False" if not
    private bool equalImpl(AbstractFunctionBase t) {
        return True;
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to rename the trigger in the database
    softlist<string> getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
        return sprintf("alter trigger %s on %s rename to %s", pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name), table_name,
            pgsql_get_sql_identifier(new_name));
    }

    #! returns normalized source for comparisons
    string getNormalizedSource(string src) {
        # remove all line comments
        src =~ s/--.*//gm;
        src = AbstractTrigger::getNormalizedSource(src);
        # remove all block comments
        src =~ s/\/\*.*\*\///g;
        # replace multiple whitespace chars with a single space
        src =~ s/\s+/ /g;
        # make everything lower case but quoted identifiers and string literals, whose case is significant
        src = pgsql_lower_unquoted(src);
        # issue #3589: PostgreSQL 12+ compatibility
        # make sure text always says "execute function" and not "execute procedure"
        src =~ s/execute function/execute procedure/;
        return src;
    }
}

#! represents a PostgreSQL-specific function
public class PgsqlFunction inherits SqlUtil::AbstractFunction {
    public {
        *string args;
    }

    constructor(string n, string n_src, *string a) : AbstractFunction(n, "function", n_src) {
        args = a;
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to create the function in the database
    softlist<string> getCreateSql(*hash opt) {
        return sprintf("create%s function %s %s", opt.replace ? " or replace" : "", name, src);
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to drop the function from the database
    /** @param opt drop options; if \a force is @ref True "True", then the \c CASCADE option will be used in the
        drop statement
    */
    string getDropSql(*hash opt) {
        return sprintf("drop function %s%s", name, opt.force ? " cascade" : "");
    }

    #! returns @ref True "True" if the argument is equal to the current object, @ref False "False" if not
    private bool equalImpl(AbstractFunctionBase t) {
        return True;
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to rename the function in the database
    /** @param new_name the new name of the object
        @param opt this argument is ignored
    */
    softlist<string> getRenameSql(string new_name, *hash opt) {
        return sprintf("alter function %s rename to %s(%s)", name, new_name, args);
    }

    #! sets the new name of the function
    setName(string new_name) {
        name = sprintf("%s(%s)", new_name, args);
    }
}

#! represents a PostgreSQL-specific trigger function
public class PgsqlTriggerFunction inherits PgsqlFunction {
    public {
        #! trigger name
        string trigger;
    }

    constructor(string n, string n_src, string n_trigger) : PgsqlFunction(n, n_src) {
        trigger = n_trigger;
    }
}

#! provides the PostgreSQL-specific implementation of the AbstractDatabase interface
public class PgsqlDatabase inherits SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase {
    public {
        #! PostgreSQL-specific schema description keys
        const PgsqlSchemaDescriptionOptions = AbstractDatabase::SchemaDescriptionOptions + (
            "types": Type::Hash,
            "type_map": Type::Hash,
        );

        const PGSQL_TempSavepoint = "qore_pgsql_tmp_savepoint";

        #! PostgreSQL native types provided by the pgvector extension
        const PgsqlVectorNativeTypes = {
            "vector": True,
            "halfvec": True,
            "sparsevec": True,
        };

        #! Options for reclaimSpace()
        const PgsqlReclaimSpaceOptions = ReclaimSpaceOptions + (
            "full"              : Type::Boolean,
            "analyze"           : Type::Boolean,
        );

        #! hash (set) of reserved words
        const PgsqlReservedWords = {
            "all": True,
            "analyse": True,
            "analyze": True,
            "and": True,
            "any": True,
            "as": True,
            "asc": True,
            "authorization": True,
            "between": True,
            "binary": True,
            "both": True,
            "case": True,
            "cast": True,
            "check": True,
            "collate": True,
            "column": True,
            "constraint": True,
            "create": True,
            "cross": True,
            "currentdate": True,
            "currenttime": True,
            "currenttimestamp": True,
            "currentuser": True,
            "default": True,
            "deferrable": True,
            "desc": True,
            "distinct": True,
            "do": True,
            "else": True,
            "end": True,
            "except": True,
            "false": True,
            "for": True,
            "foreign": True,
            "freeze": True,
            "from": True,
            "full": True,
            "grant": True,
            "group": True,
            "having": True,
            "ilike": True,
            "in": True,
            "initially": True,
            "inner": True,
            "intersect": True,
            "into": True,
            "is": True,
            "isnull": True,
            "join": True,
            "leading": True,
            "left": True,
            "like": True,
            "limit": True,
            "localtime": True,
            "localtimestamp": True,
            "natural": True,
            "new": True,
            "not": True,
            "notnull": True,
            "null": True,
            "off": True,
            "offset": True,
            "old": True,
            "on": True,
            "only": True,
            "or": True,
            "order": True,
            "outer": True,
            "overlaps": True,
            "placing": True,
            "primary": True,
            "references": True,
            "right": True,
            "select": True,
            "sessionuser": True,
            "similar": True,
            "some": True,
            "table": True,
            "then": True,
            "to": True,
            "trailing": True,
            "true": True,
            "union": True,
            "unique": True,
            "user": True,
            "using": True,
            "verbose": True,
            "when": True,
            "where": True,
        };
    }

    constructor(AbstractDatasource nds, *hash opts) : AbstractDatabase(nds, opts) {
    }

    #! returns a name as an SQL identifier for PostgreSQL
    /** A name that PostgreSQL reads as one unquoted identifier (a letter, an underscore or a non-ASCII character,
        then letters, digits, underscores, dollar signs or non-ASCII characters) and that is not a reserved word is
        returned as given, so PostgreSQL folds its letters to lower case as for any unquoted name; any other name is
        returned in double quotes with each double quote doubled, and so names exactly the object of that name

        @param name the name of a database object, without a schema

        @return the name as an SQL identifier for PostgreSQL

        @throw IDENTIFIER-ERROR the name is empty or has a NUL character

        @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.19
    */
    string getSqlIdentifier(string name) {
        return pgsql_get_sql_identifier(name);
    }

    private list<string> featuresImpl() {
        return (DB_FUNCTIONS, DB_PROCEDURES, DB_SEQUENCES,
                DB_TABLES, DB_TYPES, DB_VIEWS, DB_MVIEWS);
    }

    PgsqlType makeType(string name, string src, *hash opts) {
        return new PgsqlType(name, src);
    }

    *PgsqlType getType(string name) {
        # a name without a schema is looked up in current_schema(), as for tables; matching on the name alone
        # merged the attributes of the types of that name in every schema
        hash<PgsqlObjectName> on = pgsql_parse_object_name(name);
        *hash qh = ds.select("select attname, pg_catalog.format_type(a.atttypid, a.atttypmod) type_def "
            "from pg_class c join pg_catalog.pg_namespace n on n.oid = c.relnamespace, pg_catalog.pg_attribute a "
            "where relkind = 'c' and attrelid = c.oid AND a.attnum > 0 AND NOT a.attisdropped and relname = %v "
            "and n.nspname = %v order by attnum", on.name, on.schema ?? get_pgsql_default_schema(ds));
        if (!qh.attname)
            return;
        name = on.sql_name;

        string src = foldl $1 + ", " + $2, (map sprintf("%s %s", $1.attname, $1.type_def), qh.contextIterator());

        # replace common type names with verbose type names as stored in PostgreSQL
        foreach hash<auto> h in (PgsqlTable::PgsqlNameMap.pairIterator()) {
            src = replace(src, h.key, h.value);
        }
        # remove invalid "(1)" strings
        src = replace(src, "(1)", "");

        return new PgsqlType(name, src);
    }

    private PgsqlSequence makeSequenceImpl(string name, number start = 1, number increment = 1, *softnumber end, *hash opts) {
        return new PgsqlSequence(name, start, increment, end);
    }

    private *AbstractSequence getSequenceImpl(string name) {
        # the sequence must be looked up in the schema the name resolves in, exactly as the read below
        # resolves it.  Matching on relname alone searches every schema in the database, so with the same
        # sequence name present in another schema this reported a sequence that does not exist here and
        # then failed reading it -- or, where the read succeeded, described the WRONG schema's sequence
        hash<PgsqlObjectName> on = pgsql_parse_object_name(name);
        string schema = on.schema ?? get_pgsql_default_schema(ds);

        # the attributes of a sequence are in pg_sequence since PostgreSQL 10; before, in the sequence relation,
        # which has only its current value since.  The sequence was described with the default attributes, as the
        # columns read from the relation (start, increment and max) did not exist in any version
        *hash<auto> row;
        if (ds.getServerVersion() >= 100000) {
            row = ds.selectRow("select s.seqstart as start, s.seqincrement as increment, s.seqmax as max, "
                "s.seqmin as min, s.seqcycle as cycle from pg_catalog.pg_sequence s "
                "join pg_catalog.pg_class c on c.oid = s.seqrelid "
                "join pg_catalog.pg_namespace n on n.oid = c.relnamespace "
                "where c.relname = %v and n.nspname = %v", on.name, schema);
        } else {
            if (!ds.selectRow("select c.relname from pg_class c "
                    "join pg_namespace n on n.oid = c.relnamespace "
                    "where c.relkind = 'S' and c.relname = %v and n.nspname = %v", on.name, schema)) {
                return;
            }
            # qualified, so it cannot resolve to a same-named sequence in another schema on the search path
            row = ds.selectRow(sprintf("select start_value as start, increment_by as increment, max_value as max, "
                "min_value as min, is_cycled as cycle from %s.%s", on.sql_schema ?? pgsql_quote_identifier(schema),
                pgsql_quote_identifier(on.name)));
        }
        if (!row) {
            return;
        }

        # a maximum or minimum that is the default of a bigint sequence in its direction, as getCreateSql() creates
        # it, is not given
        bool ascending = row.increment > 0;
        *int max = row.max;
        if (max == (ascending ? 9223372036854775807 : -1)) {
            remove max;
        }
        *int min = row.min;
        if (min == (ascending ? 1 : -9223372036854775807 - 1)) {
            remove min;
        }
        return new PgsqlSequence(on.sql_name, row.start, row.increment, max, min, row.cycle);
    }

    private *AbstractView getViewImpl(string name) {
        hash<PgsqlObjectName> on = pgsql_parse_object_name(name);

        *hash<auto> row = ds.selectRow("select * from pg_views where schemaname = %v and viewname = %v",
            on.schema ?? get_pgsql_default_schema(ds), on.name);
        if (!row)
            return;

        return new PgsqlView(on.sql_name, row.definition, row.schemaname, row.viewowner);
    }

    private PgsqlFunction makeFunctionImpl(string name, string src, *hash opts) {
        string nc = name;

        trim src;

        # change $ quoting to standard form; only the tag ("$$" or "$tag$") is matched, as matching up to the last
        # "$" on the line took a body on the same line as the quotes for the tag, and replaced it
        *string dq = (src =~ x/as (\$(?:[[:alpha:]_][[:alnum:]_]*)?\$)/i)[0];
        if (dq)
            src = replace(src, dq, "$function\$");

        # move trailing "language xxx" and type to start of declaration
        if (src =~ / as /i) {
            *string type = (src =~ x/(stable|volatile|immutable)$/)[0];
            if (type)
                splice src, -(type.size() + 1);

            # only a language declaration at the end is moved; "language (.*)$" also matched one before the body
            # when the body is on the same line, and removed the body with it
            *string tlang = (src =~ x/ language (\w+)\s*$/i)[0];
            if (tlang) {
                src =~ s/ language \w+\s*$//i;
                src = regex_subst(src, " as ", " language " + tlang + " as ", RE_Caseless);
            }
            if (type)
                src = regex_subst(src, " as ", " " + type + " as ", RE_Caseless);
        }

        *string args = PgsqlDatabase::getFunctionArgs("FUNCTION-ERROR", \nc);

        if (args) {
            # remove " in " in arg list & name since it's the default and not returned by pg_get_function_identity_arguments()
            args =~ s/ in / /ig;

            # replace verbose type names with short type names
            foreach hash<auto> h in (PgsqlTable::PgsqlNameMap.pairIterator()) {
                args = replace(args, h.key, h.value);
            }

            # use our args in name
            name = regex_subst(name, "\\((.*)\\)", "(" + args + ")");
        }

        trim src;
        return new PgsqlFunction(name, src, args);
    }

    private PgsqlFunction makeProcedureImpl(string name, string src, *hash opts) {
        return makeFunctionImpl(name, src, opts);
    }

    private *AbstractFunction getFunctionImpl(string name) {
        # a name without a schema is looked up in the schema the connection resolves unqualified names in, as for
        # tables, views and sequences; matching on the function name alone found the functions of every schema,
        # so the same function in two schemas was reported as two matches, or the other schema's was described
        hash<PgsqlObjectName> on = pgsql_parse_object_name(name);
        string schema = on.schema ?? get_pgsql_default_schema(ds);
        # the function name, double-quoted or up to an argument list, and any argument list; a "(" in a quoted name
        # is part of the name
        *list<*string> fl = (on.sql_name =~ x/^("(?:[^"]|"")*+"|[^"(]+)(.*)$/s);
        # a name followed by anything but an argument list names no function
        if (!fl || (fl[1] && fl[1] !~ /^\(/)) {
            return;
        }
        # the function name as given and in the catalog
        string sql_fname = fl[0];
        name = pgsql_unquote_identifier(sql_fname);

        *hash qh;
        # try to find a unique function without arguments
        if (!fl[1]) {
            qh = ds.select("select p.proname, t1.typname return_type, a.rolname procedure_owner, l.lanname lang, "
                "prosrc body, pg_catalog.pg_get_functiondef(p.oid) hdr, "
                "pg_catalog.pg_get_function_identity_arguments(p.oid) args, "
                "case provolatile when 'i' then 'IMMUTABLE' when 's' then 'STABLE' when 'v' then 'VOLATILE' end stability "
                "from pg_proc p join pg_catalog.pg_namespace n on n.oid = p.pronamespace "
                "left join pg_type t1 on p.prorettype = t1.oid left join pg_roles a on p.proowner = a.oid "
                "left join pg_language l on p.prolang = l.oid where p.proname = %v and n.nspname = %v", name, schema);
            if (!qh.proname)
                return;
            if (qh.proname.size() > 1)
                throw "FUNCTION-ERROR", sprintf("found %d matches for function name %y: %y; use the full argument "
                    "list in the name to match only function (ex: \"%s(%s)\"", qh.proname.size(), sql_fname,
                    (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, (map sprintf("%s(%s)", sql_fname, $1.args), qh.contextIterator())),
                    sql_fname, qh.args[0]);
            # assign first row to qh
            context (qh) qh = %%;
        } else {
            string nc = fl[1];
            *string args = PgsqlDatabase::getFunctionArgs("FUNCTION-ERROR", \nc);
            nc = name;
            # replace common type names with verbose type names as stored in PostgreSQL
            if (args) {
                foreach hash<auto> h in (PgsqlTable::PgsqlNameMap.pairIterator()) {
                    args = regex_subst(args, " " + h.value + "(,|$)", " " + h.key + "$1", RE_Caseless|RE_Global);
                }
                # remove " in " in argument list since it's the default and not returned by pg_get_function_identity_arguments()
                args =~ s/ in / /ig;
            }

            qh = ds.selectRow("select p.proname, t1.typname return_type, a.rolname procedure_owner, l.lanname "
                "lang, prosrc body, pg_catalog.pg_get_functiondef(p.oid) hdr, "
                "pg_catalog.pg_get_function_identity_arguments(p.oid) args, case provolatile when 'i' then "
                "'IMMUTABLE' when 's' then 'STABLE' when 'v' then 'VOLATILE' end stability from pg_proc p "
                "join pg_catalog.pg_namespace n on n.oid = p.pronamespace "
                "left join pg_type t1 on p.prorettype = t1.oid left join pg_roles a on p.proowner = a.oid "
                "left join pg_language l on p.prolang = l.oid where p.proname = %v and n.nspname = %v and "
                "pg_catalog.pg_get_function_identity_arguments(p.oid) = %v", nc, schema, args);
            if (!qh)
                return;
        }

        # the function is named as the argument names it, without its schema
        return PgsqlDatabase::processFunction(qh + {"proname": sql_fname}, native_case);
    }

    #! returns the procedure (function) of the given name, or no value if there is none
    private *AbstractFunction getProcedureImpl(string name) {
        return getFunctionImpl(name);
    }

    static *string getFunctionArgs(string err, reference<string> src) {
        # remove function name and "(" at beginning of hdr; a "(" in a quoted identifier of the name (ex:
        # "\"my(schema\".f()") is part of the name
        *string name = (src =~ x/^((?:[^"(]++|"(?:[^"]|"")*+")*+\()/)[0];
        if (!name)
            throw err, sprintf("cannot find function arguments: %y", src);
        int i = name.length() - 1;
        int e = src.find(")", i + 1);
        if (e == -1)
            throw err, sprintf("cannot find function arguments: %y", src);

        string tmp = extract src, 0, e + 1;
        trim src;
        return extract tmp, i + 1, e - i - 1;
    }

    static PgsqlFunction processFunction(hash<auto> row, bool native_case) {
        if (!native_case)
            map row.$1 = row.$1.lwr(), ("stability");

        trim row.hdr;
        # remove "create or replace function" from the beginning of hdr
        if (row.hdr !~ /^create or replace function /i)
            throw "FUNCTION-ERROR", sprintf("function does not begin with 'create or replace function': %y", row.hdr);

        # get and remove arguments
        *string args = PgsqlDatabase::getFunctionArgs("FUNCTION-ERROR", \row.hdr);

        if (args) {
            # replace verbose type names with short type names
            foreach hash<auto> h in (PgsqlTable::PgsqlNameMap.pairIterator()) {
                args = replace(args, h.key, h.value);
            }
        }

        # fix return type
        *string rt = (row.hdr =~ x/returns (.*)/i)[0];
        if (rt) {
            string ort;
            # replace verbose type names with short type names
            foreach hash<auto> h in (PgsqlTable::PgsqlNameMap.pairIterator()) {
                rt = replace(rt, h.key, h.value);
            }
            if (ort != rt)
                row.hdr = regex_subst(row.hdr, "returns .*", "returns " + rt, RE_Caseless);
        }

        #printf("args: %y\n", args);
        if (!native_case) {
            row.hdr =~ s/^RETURNS /returns /;
            row.hdr =~ s/ SETOF / setof /;
            row.hdr =~ s/\n LANGUAGE / language /;
            row.hdr =~ s/\n STABLE/ stable/;
            row.hdr =~ s/\n IMMUTABLE/ immutable/;
            row.hdr =~ s/\nAS / as /;
        }

        string name = sprintf("%s(%s)", row.proname, args);
        return row.trigger_name ? new PgsqlTriggerFunction(name, row.hdr, row.trigger_name) : new PgsqlFunction(name, row.hdr, args);
    }

    private list getDropSchemaSqlImpl(hash schema_hash, *hash opt) {
        list l = ();

        if (opt.start) {
            return l;
        }

        list tl = dropSqlUnlocked("type", schema_hash, \getType(), \makeType(), opt, Type::String);
        if (tl) l += tl;

        return l;
    }

    private list getAlignSqlImpl(hash schema_hash, *hash opt) {
        string drv = getDriverName();
        list l = ();
        if (opt.start) {
            if (schemaUsesPgvector(schema_hash) && !pgExtensionExists("vector")) {
                l += AbstractDatabase::doCallback(opt, "create extension if not exists vector",
                    AbstractDatabase::AC_Create, "extension", "vector");
            }

            hash type_cache;

            # rename any types
            AbstractDatabase::checkDriverOptions(\schema_hash.type_map, drv);
            foreach hash<auto> th in (schema_hash.type_map.pairIterator()) {
                if (th.value.typeCode() != NT_STRING)
                    throw "SCHEMA-DESCRIPTION-ERROR", sprintf("type_map %y value is not a string in the schema description hash; got type %y instead (%y)", th.key, th.value.type(), th.value);

                *PgsqlType type = getType(th.key);
                if (type) {
                    l += AbstractDatabase::doCallback(opt, type.getRenameSql(th.value), AbstractDatabase::AC_Rename, "type", th.key, NOTHING, th.value);
                    type.name = th.value;
                    type_cache{th.value} = type;
                }
            }

            # create missing types; update different types
            AbstractDatabase::checkDriverOptions(\schema_hash.types, drv);
            foreach string name in (schema_hash.types.keyIterator()) {
                if (schema_hash.types{name}.typeCode() != NT_STRING)
                    throw "SCHEMA-DESCRIPTION-ERROR", sprintf("type %y value is not a string in the schema description hash; got type %y instead (%y)", name, schema_hash.types{name}.type(), schema_hash.types{name});

                string src = schema_hash.types{name};

                PgsqlType templateType(name, src);

                # see if type exists
                *PgsqlType type;
                if (type_cache{name})
                    type = type_cache{name};
                else {
                    type = getType(name);
                    if (!type) {
                        l += AbstractDatabase::doCallback(opt, templateType.getCreateSql(opt), AbstractDatabase::AC_Create, "type", name);
                        continue;
                    }
                }

                if (!templateType.equal(type)) {
                    l += AbstractDatabase::doCallback(opt, templateType.getModifySql(type, opt + ("force": True)), AbstractDatabase::AC_Modify, "type", name);
                }
            }
            return l;
        }
        return l;
    }

    private bool pgExtensionExists(string name) {
        *hash<auto> row = ds.selectRow("select 1 from pg_extension where extname = %v", name);
        return row ? True : False;
    }

    private bool schemaUsesPgvector(hash<auto> schema_hash) {
        return usesPgvectorType(schema_hash.tables);
    }

    private bool usesPgvectorType(auto value) {
        switch (value.typeCode()) {
            case NT_HASH: {
                hash<auto> h = value;

                if (h.native_type.typeCode() == NT_STRING
                        && PgsqlVectorNativeTypes{h.native_type.lwr()}) {
                    return True;
                }

                if (h.type.typeCode() == NT_STRING && PgsqlVectorNativeTypes{h.type.lwr()}) {
                    return True;
                }

                if (h.driver.typeCode() == NT_HASH && h.driver.pgsql.typeCode() == NT_HASH
                        && usesPgvectorType(h.driver.pgsql)) {
                    return True;
                }

                foreach hash<auto> pair in (h.pairIterator()) {
                    if (pair.key == "driver") {
                        continue;
                    }
                    if (usesPgvectorType(pair.value)) {
                        return True;
                    }
                }
                break;
            }

            case NT_LIST: {
                foreach auto elem in (value) {
                    if (usesPgvectorType(elem)) {
                        return True;
                    }
                }
                break;
            }
        }

        return False;
    }

    #! returns a list of string table names in the schemas of the connection's \c search_path
    /** Only tables in the schemas of the connection's \c search_path (<tt>current_schemas(false)</tt>) are
        listed, as Oracle and MySQL list only the tables of the connection's own schema.  A table in
        <tt>current_schema()</tt> is listed unqualified, a table in any other \c search_path schema is listed
        as \c "schema.table", so that every name returned resolves in the schema of the table listed.
        Partition child relations are not listed; views are listed as \c information_schema.tables reports them.
        An empty list is returned if the \c search_path names no existing schema.
    */
    private list<string> listTablesImpl() {
        # NOTE: exclude partition child relations. PostgreSQL exposes each declarative partition as its own
        # relation, but a partition is not a standalone logical table and must not appear in the table list
        # (Oracle and MySQL already behave this way). A partition child is a pg_inherits child whose parent is a
        # partitioned table (pg_class.relkind = 'p'). This check is version-safe (on servers without declarative
        # partitioning the 'p' relkind simply never matches) and does not exclude classic table-inheritance
        # children, whose parent relkind is 'r'.
        # NOTE: listing every schema of the database exposed other schemas' tables and, in a database with
        # one schema per tenant, repeated the same table names once per schema
        string sql = sprintf("select %s as name from information_schema.tables t "
            "where t.table_schema = any (current_schemas(false)) "
            "and not exists (select 1 from pg_catalog.pg_inherits inh "
            "join pg_catalog.pg_class child on child.oid = inh.inhrelid "
            "join pg_catalog.pg_namespace cn on cn.oid = child.relnamespace "
            "join pg_catalog.pg_class parent on parent.oid = inh.inhparent "
            "where cn.nspname = t.table_schema and child.relname = t.table_name and parent.relkind = 'p')",
            pgsql_relation_name_expr("t.table_schema", "t.table_name"));
        return ds.select(sql).name ?? ();
    }

    #! returns a list of string function names in the schemas of the connection's \c search_path
    /** The function names will include arguments in parentheses after the names; names are qualified as for
        tables (see listTablesImpl()): a function in <tt>current_schema()</tt> is listed by its name alone, a
        function in any other \c search_path schema as \c "schema.name(args)"

        @return a list of string function names in the schemas of the connection's \c search_path
    */
    private list<string> listFunctionsImpl() {
        # the functions of the search_path schemas; this listed the functions of the "public" schema whatever the
        # connection's search_path, so the functions of any other schema could not be listed
        string sql = sprintf("select n.nspname as schema, %s as name, pg_catalog.pg_get_function_result(p.oid) "
            "as result_type, pg_catalog.pg_get_function_arguments(p.oid) as args,"
            " case when p.prokind = 'a' then 'agg' when p.prokind = 'w' then 'window' when p.prorettype = 'pg_catalog.trigger'::pg_catalog.regtype then 'trigger' else 'normal' end as type"
            " from pg_catalog.pg_proc p join pg_catalog.pg_namespace n on n.oid = p.pronamespace"
            " where n.nspname = any (current_schemas(false)) order by 1, 2, 4",
            pgsql_relation_name_expr("n.nspname", "p.proname"));
        *hash qh = ds.select(sql);

        return map sprintf("%s(%s)", $1.name, $1.args), qh.contextIterator();
    }

    #! since PostgreSQL only supports functions, this method is identical to listFunctionsImpl()
    /** @see listFunctionsImpl()
    */
    private list<string> listProceduresImpl() {
        return listFunctionsImpl();
    }

    #! returns a list of string sequence names in the schemas of the connection's \c search_path
    /** Names are qualified as for tables; see listTablesImpl()
    */
    private list<string> listSequencesImpl() {
        # only the sequences reachable on the search path; matching on relkind alone lists every schema's
        # sequences, so in a database holding more than one schema this reported sequences that unqualified
        # SQL here cannot reach.  A sequence outside current_schema() is qualified, as getSequence() resolves
        # an unqualified name in current_schema() only
        return ds.select(sprintf("select %s as name from pg_catalog.pg_class c "
            "join pg_catalog.pg_namespace n on n.oid = c.relnamespace "
            "where c.relkind = 'S' and n.nspname = any (current_schemas(false)) order by 1",
            pgsql_relation_name_expr("n.nspname", "c.relname"))).name ?? ();
    }

    #! returns a list of string view names in the schemas of the connection's \c search_path
    /** Names are qualified as for tables; see listTablesImpl()
    */
    private list<string> listViewsImpl() {
        # a view outside current_schema() is qualified, as getView() resolves an unqualified name in
        # current_schema() only, and the same view name in two search_path schemas must not be listed twice
        return ds.select(sprintf("select %s as name from information_schema.views v "
            "where v.table_schema = any (current_schemas(false)) order by 1",
            pgsql_relation_name_expr("v.table_schema", "v.table_name"))).name ?? ();
    }

    #! returns a list of string materialized view names in the schemas of the connection's \c search_path
    /** Names are qualified as for tables; see @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::listTables() "listTables()"

        @return a list of string materialized view names in the schemas of the connection's \c search_path;
        a materialized view in <tt>current_schema()</tt> is listed unqualified, one in any other \c search_path
        schema as \c "schema.name"
    */
    list<string> listMaterializedViews() {
        # relkind 'm' is a materialized view; this previously listed relkind 'r' (ordinary tables) of every
        # schema in the database
        return ds.select(sprintf("select %s as name from pg_catalog.pg_class c "
            "join pg_catalog.pg_namespace n on n.oid = c.relnamespace "
            "where c.relkind = 'm' and n.nspname = any (current_schemas(false)) order by 1",
            pgsql_relation_name_expr("n.nspname", "c.relname"))).name ?? ();
    }

    #! returns an iterator listing the string materialized view names in the database
    ListIterator<string> materializedViewIterator() {
        return listMaterializedViews().iterator();
    }

    #! returns a list of string type names in the schemas of the connection's \c search_path
    /** Names are qualified as for tables (see @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::listTables() "listTables()"): a type
        in <tt>current_schema()</tt> is listed by its name alone, a type in any other \c search_path schema as
        \c "schema.name"

        @return a list of string type names in the schemas of the connection's \c search_path
    */
    list<string> listTypes() {
        # this listed the types of every schema in the database, so the same type name in two schemas was listed
        # twice, unqualified
        return ds.select(sprintf("select %s as name from information_schema.user_defined_types t "
            "where t.user_defined_type_schema = any (current_schemas(false)) order by 1",
            pgsql_relation_name_expr("t.user_defined_type_schema", "t.user_defined_type_name"))).name ?? ();
    }

    #! returns an iterator listing the string type names in the database
    ListIterator<string> typeIterator() {
        return listTypes().iterator();
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to create the schema in the database
    private string getCreateSqlImpl(list l) {
        return PgsqlDatabase::getCreateSql(l);
    }

    #! returns a string that can be used to create the schema in the database
    static string getCreateSql(list l) {
        return !l ? "" : foldl $1 + "\n" + $2, (map sprintf("%s%s", $1, $1 =~ /(;|\$)$/ ? "" : ";"), l);
    }

    #! returns driver-specific options to the base abstract class
    private hash getSchemaDescriptionOptions() {
        return PgsqlSchemaDescriptionOptions;
    }

    #! returns driver-specific options to the base abstract class
    private hash getReclaimSpaceOptions() {
        return PgsqlReclaimSpaceOptions;
    }

    #! returns the next value in the given sequence
    private softint getNextSequenceValueImpl(string name) {
        # nextval() reads the name as SQL names a relation, so a name that needs quotes is given quoted
        return ds.selectRow("select nextval(%v)", pgsql_get_sql_object_name(name)).nextval;
    }

    #! returns the last value issued for the given sequence in the current session
    private softint getCurrentSequenceValueImpl(string name) {
        return ds.selectRow("select last_value from " + pgsql_get_sql_object_name(name)).last_value;
    }

    #! returns @ref True "True" if the database supports sequences
    private bool supportsSequencesImpl() {
        return True;
    }

    #! returns @ref True "True" as PostgreSQL supports temporary tables (\c "create temporary table")
    private bool supportsTempTablesImpl() {
        return True;
    }

    #! returns the physical name for a temporary table: a \c "pg_temp"-qualified name
    /** PostgreSQL creates temporary tables in a session-private schema (\c "pg_temp_N") that is never the
        connection's default schema, so an unqualified temporary-table name would be qualified with the
        connection's default schema (e.g. \c "public") in generated SQL and fail to resolve; the \c "pg_temp"
        alias always refers to the calling session's own temporary schema, so the physical name is qualified
        with it explicitly.  Pre-created temporary tables are assumed to already carry the correct physical
        name and are returned unchanged
    */
    private string getTempTableNameImpl(string name, bool precreated) {
        if (precreated || name =~ /^pg_temp\./) {
            return name;
        }
        if (name =~ /\./) {
            throw "TEMP-TABLE-ERROR", sprintf("cannot create temporary table %y: PostgreSQL temporary tables "
                "cannot be created in an explicit schema; use an unqualified name (the table is created in the "
                "session's temporary schema)", name);
        }
        return "pg_temp." + name;
    }

    #! returns @ref True "True" if the database supports named types
    private bool supportsTypesImpl() {
        return True;
    }

    #! returns @ref True "True" if the database supports packages
    private bool supportsPackagesImpl() {
        return False;
    }

    #! rebuild index implementation. See @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::rebuildIndex()
    private bool rebuildIndexImpl(string name, *hash options) {
        string sql = sprintf("reindex index %s", pgsql_get_sql_object_name(name));
        ds.select(sql);
        return True;
    }

    #! compute statistics implementation. See @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::computeStatistics()
    private computeStatisticsImpl(*hash options) {
        softlist tables = options.tables ? options.tables : listTables();

        foreach string i in (tables) {
            # "%s" in SQL with arguments is substituted as given, so the table name is quoted here
            ds.select("analyze " + pgsql_get_sql_object_name(i));
        }
    }

    #! reclaim space implementation. See @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::reclaimSpace()
    private reclaimSpaceImpl(*hash options) {
        softlist tables = options.tables ? options.tables : listTables();

        string sql = sprintf("vacuum %s%s", options.full ? "full " : "", options.analyze ? "analyze " : "");

        if (options.tables) {
            # each table is vacuumed by its name; the SQL had no place for the name, so every table of the
            # database was vacuumed once for each table given
            foreach string i in (tables) {
                ds.select(sql + pgsql_get_sql_object_name(i));
            }
        } else {
            ds.select(sql);
        }
    }

    private *string getDbName() {
        return ds.getDBName();
    }

    #! @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::getPhysicalSize()
    private int getPhysicalSizeImpl() {
        *string dbname = getDbName();
        if (!exists dbname) {
            return GET_PHYSICAL_DB_SIZE_NOVAL;
        }
        return ds.selectRow("select pg_database_size(%v) as val", dbname).val ?? GET_PHYSICAL_DB_SIZE_NOVAL;
    }

    #! @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::getSchemaPhysicalSize()
    private int getSchemaPhysicalSizeImpl(string schema) {
        *hash<auto> row = ds.selectRow(getNamespaceSizeSql("%v"), schema);
        if (!row) {
            throw "SCHEMA-NOT-FOUND", sprintf("PostgreSQL schema %y does not exist", schema);
        }
        return row.size_bytes;
    }

    #! @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::getCurrentSchemaPhysicalSize()
    /** The current schema is \c current_schema(): the first existing schema in the connection's \c search_path;
        it is determined in the same statement that measures it

        @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.19
    */
    private int getCurrentSchemaPhysicalSizeImpl() {
        # no row: no schema in the search_path exists
        return ds.selectRow(getNamespaceSizeSql("current_schema()")).size_bytes ?? GET_PHYSICAL_DB_SIZE_NOVAL;
    }

    #! Returns the SQL that measures the physical size of one schema, given by a SQL expression
    /** @param schema_expr the SQL expression giving the schema name, such as a placeholder or \c current_schema()

        @return a select statement giving \c size_bytes for the schema, or no row if the schema does not exist

        @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.19
    */
    private static string getNamespaceSizeSql(string schema_expr) {
        # Enumerate only storage roots in the requested namespace.  pg_total_relation_size() already includes
        # every table/materialized-view index and its TOAST relation and index.  Enumerating relkind 'i', 'I',
        # or the pg_toast namespace separately would therefore double count storage.  Partitioned roots have no
        # storage; each physical leaf is relkind 'r' and is counted independently.  Only the relations of the
        # namespace are measured, whatever the size of other schemas or of the database.
        return "select coalesce(sum(case "
            "when c.relkind in ('r', 'm') then pg_catalog.pg_total_relation_size(c.oid) "
            "when c.relkind = 'S' then pg_catalog.pg_relation_size(c.oid) "
            "else 0 end), 0)::bigint as size_bytes "
            "from pg_catalog.pg_namespace n "
            "left join pg_catalog.pg_class c on c.relnamespace = n.oid "
            "and c.relkind in ('r', 'm', 'S') "
            "where n.nspname = " + schema_expr + " group by n.oid";
    }

    #! tries to execute a command so that if an error occurs the current transaction status is not lost
    private auto tryExecArgsImpl(string sql, *softlist<auto> args) {
        return PgsqlDatabase::tryExecArgs(ds, sql, args);
    }

    #! tries to execute a command so that if an error occurs the current transaction status is not lost
    private auto tryExecRawImpl(string sql, *softlist<auto> args) {
        return PgsqlDatabase::tryExecRaw(ds, sql);
    }

    #! tries to execute a command so that if an error occurs the current transaction status is not lost
    static auto tryExecArgs(AbstractDatasource ds, string sql, *softlist<auto> args) {
        ds.exec("savepoint " + PGSQL_TempSavepoint);

        auto rv;
        try {
            rv = ds.vexec(sql, args);
            ds.exec("release savepoint " + PGSQL_TempSavepoint);
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            ds.exec("rollback to savepoint " + PGSQL_TempSavepoint);
            rethrow;
        }
        return rv;
    }

    #! tries to execute a command so that if an error occurs the current transaction status is not lost
    static auto tryExecRaw(AbstractDatasource ds, string sql) {
        ds.exec("savepoint " + PGSQL_TempSavepoint);

        auto rv;
        try {
            rv = ds.execRaw(sql);
            ds.exec("release savepoint " + PGSQL_TempSavepoint);
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            ds.exec("rollback to savepoint " + PGSQL_TempSavepoint);
            rethrow;
        }
        return rv;
    }
}

#! provides the PostgreSQL-specific implementation of the SqlUtil::AbstractTable interface
public class PgsqlTable inherits SqlUtil::AbstractTable {
    public {
        #! maps PostgreSQL type names to type configurations
        const PgsqlTypeMap = (
            "abstime": ("qore": Type::Date,),
            "bigint": ("qore": Type::Int,),
            "bigserial": ("qore": Type::Int,),
            "bit": ("qore": Type::Binary, "size": SZ_OPT,),
            "bit varying": ("qore": Type::Binary, "size": SZ_OPT,),
            "bool": ("qore": Type::Boolean,),
            "boolean": ("qore": Type::Boolean,),
            "bytea": ("qore": Type::Binary,),
            "char": ("qore": Type::String, "size": SZ_OPT,),
            "character": ("qore": Type::String, "size": SZ_OPT,),
            "character varying": ("qore": Type::String, "size": SZ_OPT,),
            "cidr": ("qore": Type::String,),
            "date": ("qore": Type::Date,),
            "decimal": ("qore": Type::Number, "size": SZ_NUM,),
            "double precision": ("qore": Type::Float,),
            "float": ("qore": Type::Float,),
            "inet": ("qore": Type::String,),
            "int": ("qore": Type::Int,),
            "integer": ("qore": Type::Int,),
            "interval": ("qore": Type::Date, "size": SZ_OPT, "size_range": (0, 6), "default_size": 6,),
            "json": {"qore": Type::String},
            "jsonb": {"qore": Type::String},
            "macaddr": ("qore": Type::String,),
            "money": ("qore": Type::Number,),
            "numeric": ("qore": Type::Number, "size": SZ_NUM,),
            "oid": ("qore": Type::Int,),
            "real": ("qore": Type::Float,),
            "reltime": ("qore": Type::Date,),
            "serial": ("qore": Type::Int,),
            "smallint": ("qore": Type::Int,),
            "smallserial": ("qore": Type::Int,),
            "text": ("qore": Type::String,),
            "time": ("qore": Type::Date, "size": SZ_OPT, "size_range": (0, 6), "default_size": 6,),
            "time with time zone": ("qore": Type::Date, "size": SZ_OPT, "size_range": (0, 6), "default_size": 6,),
            "time without time zone": ("qore": Type::Date, "size": SZ_OPT, "size_range": (0, 6), "default_size": 6,),
            "timetz": ("qore": Type::Date, "size": SZ_OPT, "size_range": (0, 6), "default_size": 6,),
            "timestamp": ("qore": Type::Date, "size": SZ_OPT, "size_range": (0, 6), "default_size": 6,),
            "timestamp with time zone": ("qore": Type::Date, "size": SZ_OPT, "size_range": (0, 6), "default_size": 6,),
            "timestamp without time zone": ("qore": Type::Date, "size": SZ_OPT, "size_range": (0, 6), "default_size": 6,),
            "timestamptz": ("qore": Type::Date, "size": SZ_OPT, "size_range": (0, 6), "default_size": 6,),
            "varbit": ("qore": Type::Binary, "size": SZ_OPT,),
            "varchar": ("qore": Type::String, "size": SZ_OPT,),
            "xml": {"qore": Type::String},
            # pgvector extension types
            "vector": ("qore": Type::List, "size": SZ_OPT,),
            "halfvec": ("qore": Type::List, "size": SZ_OPT,),
            "sparsevec": ("qore": Type::Hash, "size": SZ_OPT,),
        );

        #! the driver option giving the time zone used to convert date/time values
        const TimeZoneOption = "timezone";

        #! PostgreSQL date/time type names, in the simple form returned by @ref normalizePgTypeName()
        /** used to interpret a partition bound literal that declares its own type when the partition key has no
            column metadata

            @since PgsqlSqlUtilBase 1.10
        */
        const PgPartitionDateTypes = {
            "date": True,
            "timestamp": True,
            "timestamptz": True,
        };

        #! maps from verbose type names to simple type names
        const PgsqlNameMap = (
            "bit varying": "varbit",
            "character": "char",
            "character varying": "varchar",
            "char varying": "varchar",
            "double precision": "float",
            "time with time zone": "timetz",
            "time without time zone": "time",
            "timestamp with time zone": "timestamptz",
            "timestamp without time zone": "timestamp",
        );

        #! maps qore type names to PostgreSQL type names
        # note: \c Type::Float maps to the short name \c "float" (not \c "double precision")
        # so fresh column definitions match what \c getColumnsImpl reads back — \c PgsqlNameMap
        # rewrites \c "double precision" to \c "float" for read-back, and having the forward
        # path emit the same short name avoids spurious "alter column type" diffs on every
        # alignment of a schema with floating-point columns
        const QoreTypeMap = (
            "integer": "bigint",
            "float": "float",
            "number": "numeric",
            "string": "varchar",
            "date": "timestamp",
            "binary": "bytea",
            "bool": "boolean",
            SqlUtil::CHAR: "char",
            SqlUtil::CLOB: "text",
            SqlUtil::BLOB: "bytea",
        );

        #! extends @ref SqlUtil::AbstractTable::TableDescriptionHashOptions with \c "functions"
        /** for table functions required by triggers on the table
        */
        const PgsqlTableDescriptionHashOptions = AbstractTable::TableDescriptionHashOptions + (
            "functions": Type::Hash,
        );

        const PgsqlColumnDescOptions = AbstractTable::ColumnDescOptions;

        const PgsqlIndexOptions = AbstractTable::IndexOptions + {
            "where": Type::String,
            "include": Type::List,
            "nulls_not_distinct": Type::Boolean,
            "order": Type::Hash,
            "nulls": Type::Hash,
        };

        const PgsqlConstraintOptions = AbstractTable::ConstraintOptions + (
            "index": Type::String,
        );

        const PgsqlTableCreationOptions = AbstractTable::TableCreationOptions + PgsqlConstraintOptions
            + PgsqlIndexOptions + (
            "omit_trigger_functions": Type::Boolean,
        );

        const PgsqlAlignTableOptions = AbstractTable::AlignTableOptions + PgsqlTableCreationOptions;

        #! Expression map for PostgreSQL
        const PgsqlExpressionMap = DefaultExpressionMap + {
            # PostgreSQL server-side digest: sha2 family via the built-in shaNNN(bytea) functions
            # (no pgcrypto required) and md5 via the built-in md5(text) function; sha1 is not a
            # built-in (it requires the pgcrypto extension) so it is rejected here.
            DP_FUNC_DIGEST: DefaultExpressionMap{DP_FUNC_DIGEST} + {
                "code": string sub (reference<hash<QueryInfo>> info, int role, list<auto> args) {
                    string alg = sqlutil_normalize_digest_algorithm(args[1]);
                    string enc = sqlutil_normalize_digest_encoding(args[2]);
                    string v = info.table.getExpressionArg(\info, role, args[0],
                        AbstractDataProviderTypeMap."string");
                    string core;
                    switch (alg) {
                        case "md5":
                            core = sprintf("decode(md5(%s), 'hex')", v);
                            break;
                        case "sha224":
                        case "sha256":
                        case "sha384":
                        case "sha512":
                            core = sprintf("%s(convert_to(%s, 'UTF8'))", alg, v);
                            break;
                        default:
                            throw "DIGEST-ERROR", sprintf("digest algorithm %y is not supported by the "
                                "PostgreSQL driver; supported algorithms: md5, sha224, sha256, sha384, "
                                "sha512", alg);
                    }
                    switch (enc) {
                        case "binary":
                            return core;
                        case "hex":
                            return sprintf("encode(%s, 'hex')", core);
                        case "base64":
                            # strip the newlines PostgreSQL inserts into base64 output every 76 chars
                            return sprintf("translate(encode(%s, 'base64'), chr(10), '')", core);
                    }
                    throw "DIGEST-ERROR", sprintf("invalid digest output encoding %y", enc);
                },
            },
            COP_CAST: {
                "exp": <DataProviderExpressionInfo>{
                    "type": DET_Operator,
                    "name": COP_CAST,
                    "display_name": "cast",
                    "desc": "cast operator",
                    "symbol": "cast",
                    "groups": "Data Conversion",
                    "args": (
                        DataProviderSignatureAnyType,
                        DataProviderSignatureStringValueType,
                        DataProviderSignatureOptionalIntValueType,
                        DataProviderSignatureOptionalIntValueType,
                    ),
                    "return_type": AbstractDataProviderTypeMap."any",
                },
                "code": string sub (reference<hash<QueryInfo>> info, int role, list<auto> args) {
                    string exp0 = info.table.getExpressionArg(\info, role, args[0],
                        AbstractDataProviderTypeMap."any");
                    # the arguments are the expression, the type, and an optional size and scale
                    string name = QoreTypeMap{args[1]} ?? args[1];
                    *hash<auto> desc = PgsqlTypeMap{name};
                    string sql = sprintf ("cast (%s as %s", exp0, name);
                    if (desc.size == SZ_MAND) {
                        if (!exists args[2] && !exists desc.size_range)
                            throw "OPERATOR-ERROR", sprintf ("cast operator missing size for type %s",
                                name);
                        sql += sprintf("(%d)", args[2] ?? desc.size_range[1]);
                    } else if (desc.size == SZ_NUM && exists args[2]) {
                        sql += sprintf("(%d", args[2]);
                        sql += exists args[3] ? sprintf(",%d)", args[3]) : ")";
                    } else if (desc.size == SZ_OPT && exists args[2]) {
                        sql += sprintf("(%d)", args[2]);
                    }
                    sql += ")";
                    return sql;
                },
            },
            COP_YEAR: {
                "exp": <DataProviderExpressionInfo>{
                    "type": DET_Operator,
                    "name": COP_YEAR,
